00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.320
AMY BANZAERT: Welcome.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.118
I'm really excited.

00:00:28.118 --> 00:00:30.160
This is the second year
of this class [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.452
and that I've been teaching it.

00:00:31.452 --> 00:00:34.570
So hopefully a little
bit of the growing pains

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:35.620
have been worked out.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.640
It should be a fun, good class.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.960
So today, we're going
to talk about a bunch

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:41.980
of different stuff.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.530
It's all kind of high level,
and most of this class

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.080
is pretty low-level detail.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.670
And this is high
level to give you

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.930
the macro view before we dive
into the more detailed stuff.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.240
The way I think about it
is it's like a toolbox

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.400
of stuff you'll need
every day in this class,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.610
either in the back of your
mind or the front, depending.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.800
So it's just these
random bits and pieces.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:03.550
And I apologize.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.800
There's no good
way to make it be

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.052
any more cohesive than it is.

00:01:07.052 --> 00:01:08.510
But hopefully,
it'll hang together.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:10.510
So we're going to talk
about energy in the world

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:16.360
generally, review units,
review a little bit of EE,

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.820
do a little bit of estimation
practice, which we'll

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.270
do almost every time
we meet, and then

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:27.550
just talk about the class and
homework and stuff like that.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.970
And I forgot my
dongle today, so I'm

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.280
working off of two computers.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.520
I apologize if that
gets too crazy.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.710
OK, so if you can
grab the document that

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:46.870
has a graph on the
top-left corner,

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:51.100
we're going to go
through what life

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.530
is like using a bunch
of different development

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.610
indicators we got from the World
Bank in different countries.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.370
And the countries I
picked are Nicaragua

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.590
because that's the country
where this class travels

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.290
to over spring break--

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.262
and I'll talk more
about that later--

00:02:06.262 --> 00:02:08.470
Haiti because it's the
poorest country in the Western

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:09.603
Hemisphere--

00:02:09.603 --> 00:02:11.770
and so pretty close, and
it's been in the news a lot

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.230
because of some of the
tragedies they've had.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.840
United Arab Emirates
because this class

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.870
is also taught at Masdar
Institute of Science

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:23.590
and Technology there and is also
a differently very developed

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.510
nation; the US
because that's where

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.370
we are, China because they're
also in the news a lot,

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:31.300
and then the DR
Congo, which the--

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.370
deciding who is the poorest
country in the world

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.500
is a kind of impossible
task because it depends

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.350
on what you count what
it is, but DR Congo

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:40.430
ranks as one of them.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.470
So you can see already there's
some pretty severe differences

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.120
in these different
countries in terms

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.700
of the population,
the service area,

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.270
and then the resultant
population density, which

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.250
you can imagine is going
to have a big impact

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:55.820
on the quality of life.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.630
So I'll leave it at
that, and then we'll

00:02:58.630 --> 00:02:59.900
draw conclusions at the end.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:06.632
So now we're going to do what
I would call a ludicrous quiz

00:03:06.632 --> 00:03:08.090
because it's going
to be difficult.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.930
So the way you do this and
we're going to do together

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:14.290
is first try to guess what
the biggest number should

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:15.770
be on the y-axis.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:20.710
So what's the largest GNI
per capita in US dollars?

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:23.610
And throw out some numbers.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:28.300
GNI is the average
income a person makes,

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.635
Gross National Income.

00:03:31.635 --> 00:03:32.590
AUDIENCE: $30k.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.740
AMY BANZAERT: So $30k.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.610
Anyone want to say higher?

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.530
AUDIENCE: $45k.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:38.590
AMY BANZAERT: 45.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:43.620
Can we go higher, lower?

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.545
OK, and what do you think
the worst-case scenario is?

00:03:46.545 --> 00:03:49.428
What are the poorest
people making?

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:50.280
AUDIENCE: $5.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.560
AMY BANZAERT: $5?

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.740
Anyone more optimistic
than $5 per year?

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.140
OK.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:04.280
And then best guess on who is
going to be the one up here?

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:11.645
This should be fast--

00:04:11.645 --> 00:04:12.110
AUDIENCE: US.

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:12.590
AMY BANZAERT: --fun.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:13.400
I won't judge you.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.057
[LAUGHS]

00:04:15.057 --> 00:04:16.640
AMY BANZAERT: All
right, guess for US.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.610
Anyone want to
guess someone else?

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:20.883
OK, and the poorest?

00:04:20.883 --> 00:04:21.550
AUDIENCE: Haiti.

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:22.430
AUDIENCE: Haiti.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.260
AMY BANZAERT: Haiti.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:29.960
OK, so now-- no, that one.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:34.430
All right, so in
reality, US, yup,

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.220
you guys did great with those.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:43.790
$5 is a little low by an
order of magnitude or two.

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:47.295
And Haiti is the poorest country
in the Western Hemisphere

00:04:47.295 --> 00:04:48.170
but not in the world.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.660
That would be Congo
or one of them.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:56.098
And so, yeah, they're
in fact, the lowest.

00:04:56.098 --> 00:04:57.890
All right, so now it's
going to get harder.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:06.710
So what percentage of people
have improved access to water?

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.960
This one, we don't have
to worry about the y-axis

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.040
because we're talking
about percentages.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.920
So we we're at 100%.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:19.310
So let's take-- who do you
think has the best water quality

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:19.912
access?

00:05:19.912 --> 00:05:21.620
And what percentage
of people have access

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.142
to clean water in that country?

00:05:24.142 --> 00:05:25.890
AUDIENCE: I'd say it's the US.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.440
AMY BANZAERT: The
US, and how much?

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:28.880
AUDIENCE: 99%.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.500
AMY BANZAERT: 99%,
so basically 100%.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:37.440
Anyone want to be a different
country or more pessimistic?

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:37.940
OK.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:39.657
AUDIENCE: Maybe a
little less than 100%.

00:05:39.657 --> 00:05:41.240
AMY BANZAERT: A
little less than 100%?

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.445
OK, and then who do you
think has the poorest?

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:47.217
AUDIENCE: Congo.

00:05:47.217 --> 00:05:48.020
AUDIENCE: Congo.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.020
AMY BANZAERT: The Congo?

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:49.920
OK.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.070
So let's go check this out.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:59.530
So the UAE actually beats the
US in terms of clean water.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.625
And we actually forgot
to ask the percent.

00:06:02.625 --> 00:06:05.680
I forgot to ask the
percentage, so oops on that.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.170
But I think, generally, it's
higher than people guess.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:13.530
However, these
numbers are always

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:16.470
complicated in terms of what
does that actually mean.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:20.663
And does having access to clean
water mean 100% of the time?

00:06:20.663 --> 00:06:22.080
Are the people
collecting the data

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.490
actually getting good data?

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.470
Or is there a bit
of cheating going

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.700
on to make your country
look better than it is,

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:28.570
which happens a lot?

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:32.108
So these aren't necessarily
things to be confident on.

00:06:32.108 --> 00:06:33.900
One of the things I
find really interesting

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.660
is that China and Nicaragua
are almost at the same level.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.150
And Nicaragua is a much,
much poorer country

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:43.710
in a lot of other
measures than China.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.320
OK, next, improved sanitation.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:53.040
So who do you think has
the best and at what level?

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.780
AUDIENCE: Probably UAE again.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.600
AMY BANZAERT: UAE, and how much?

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:58.560
AUDIENCE: 90%.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.050
AMY BANZAERT: 90%?

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.520
UAE.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.640
Anyone else?

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.520
This is a class where
it's great to participate,

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.400
and you don't need to be
shy about guessing wrong

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:11.750
because we're all guessing here.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:13.220
This is not a type
of thing you're

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:14.690
going to know off
the top of your head.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:15.190
Yeah?

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.083
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
improved sanitation?

00:07:17.083 --> 00:07:18.500
AMY BANZAERT:
Improved sanitation.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.530
So unimproved sanitation
is you poop in a hole,

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:25.340
and maybe that hole
goes down to the river

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.080
and pollutes your river.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.750
Maybe it's behind your
backyard and just sits there.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:32.330
Maybe you don't
even have a hole.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.760
So this would be
improved to a level

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:41.300
that the World Bank considers
it actually making a difference

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:42.663
in terms of people's health.

00:07:42.663 --> 00:07:44.330
And if you go to the
World Bank website,

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:48.440
it explains it more succinctly
and more tactically than I can.

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:50.693
AUDIENCE: So would it
include [INAUDIBLE]??

00:07:50.693 --> 00:07:51.610
AMY BANZAERT: Exactly.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.440
So and again, this
is for urban access,

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.550
not for rural access
because, in rural areas,

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:59.710
you can have a deep
hole, and that'll

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.260
work fine if it's dug
in the right place,

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:04.360
whereas if you are in
an urban environment,

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.030
there's just too much population
density for that to work.

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:13.430
So any other guesses
for best case?

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:14.132
AUDIENCE: US.

00:08:14.132 --> 00:08:15.590
AMY BANZAERT: The
US, and how much?

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:16.430
AUDIENCE: 99%.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:18.800
AMY BANZAERT: 99%,
OK, and then what

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.210
do you think the low bar is?

00:08:22.863 --> 00:08:23.530
AUDIENCE: Haiti.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:24.370
AUDIENCE: Haiti.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:26.620
AMY BANZAERT: 80%?

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:27.640
Oh, you said Haiti?

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:28.307
AUDIENCE: Haiti.

00:08:28.307 --> 00:08:31.210
AMY BANZAERT: Haiti,
OK, and I'm sorry.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:34.179
And these numbers are all
from before the earthquake,

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:36.820
so it's worse now in Haiti.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.730
And what percentage
do you think in Haiti?

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:40.448
AUDIENCE: 50%.

00:08:40.448 --> 00:08:40.809
AUDIENCE: 50%.

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:41.559
AMY BANZAERT: 50%?

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:50.600
OK, so, 50%, 30%, 20%,
somewhere in there.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:51.100
OK.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:58.002
So Haiti does
indeed win this one,

00:08:58.002 --> 00:08:59.460
and this was before
the earthquake,

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.710
so it's worse now, as I said.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:04.800
A lot of that has to do
with population density

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.957
that Haiti is just so dense
that it's really hard to manage.

00:09:07.957 --> 00:09:09.540
There's more people
in the urban areas

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:11.640
than there are in
most other countries.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:14.910
And here, the USA is
a little bit better

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:18.150
than the UAE in
terms of sanitation.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.820
There's a sheet that I'll hand
out that has all of these,

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.480
so you can write it
down if you want,

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:25.740
but you don't have to
frantically record everything.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:28.320
Sorry to not warn
you of that earlier.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:35.260
OK, so energy use in
kilograms of oil per person--

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:38.160
so now you don't
get the easy 100%.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:40.380
So these are getting
harder, as I mentioned.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.200
So how many kilograms
of oil do you

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.348
think a person in the US uses?

00:09:45.348 --> 00:09:46.140
AUDIENCE: Per year?

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.098
AMY BANZAERT: Per year.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:52.345
AUDIENCE: 300.

00:09:52.345 --> 00:09:53.095
AMY BANZAERT: 300?

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.345
Price is Right, Hi Lo, anyone?

00:09:59.345 --> 00:10:00.220
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:01.270
AMY BANZAERT: All right,
throw out a number.

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:02.200
AUDIENCE: 1,000.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.690
AMY BANZAERT: 1,000?

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:07.360
Anyone want to go up an
order of magnitude or down?

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.410
So by that, I mean
going up to 10,000

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:13.915
instead of 1,000 or 100,000.

00:10:13.915 --> 00:10:16.040
When it's just crazy numbers
where you don't really

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.890
have a good sense, it's kind of
meaningless to say, 300 or 400.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:23.660
They're all as meaningful as
10 or 100 or 1,000 or 100,000.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:26.930
Any other-- are guys
happy with this?

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.260
OK, UAE, USA, someone else?

00:10:34.256 --> 00:10:35.180
AUDIENCE: China.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:36.013
AMY BANZAERT: China?

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:39.493
Anyone else?

00:10:39.493 --> 00:10:40.035
AUDIENCE: US.

00:10:40.035 --> 00:10:40.743
AUDIENCE: The US.

00:10:40.743 --> 00:10:42.410
AUDIENCE: US.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.110
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:46.610
AUDIENCE: UAE has oil, and it
doesn't have that many people,

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:47.720
so technically--

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:49.678
AMY BANZAERT: So they
could be using a lot too,

00:10:49.678 --> 00:10:51.313
so those are all possibilities.

00:10:51.313 --> 00:10:52.730
All right, on the
low end, what do

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:56.142
you think the lowest number
might be of kilograms of oil?

00:10:56.142 --> 00:10:57.309
AUDIENCE: Congo [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:57.309 --> 00:10:58.309
AMY BANZAERT: I'm sorry?

00:10:58.309 --> 00:10:59.070
AUDIENCE: Congo.

00:10:59.070 --> 00:10:59.460
AMY BANZAERT: 100?

00:10:59.460 --> 00:10:59.880
AUDIENCE: Congo.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:00.720
AUDIENCE: Congo.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.302
AMY BANZAERT:
Congo, and how much?

00:11:02.302 --> 00:11:03.126
AUDIENCE: 12.

00:11:03.126 --> 00:11:03.950
AUDIENCE: 10.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:04.658
AMY BANZAERT: 10?

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:09.100
Anyone want to go to 1 or 100?

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:10.372
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:10.372 --> 00:11:11.080
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:22.260
So there's a lot
that we use, way off

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:23.460
by an order of magnitude.

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:27.270
We're at about 10,000
is where we are.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.940
Again, these are a few years
old, so China is catching up,

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:34.860
but it's nowhere near where
the US or the UAE is yet.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:38.195
And then Haiti and
DR Congo were-- you

00:11:38.195 --> 00:11:39.820
guys were low by an
order of magnitude.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:41.195
So even in the
poorest countries,

00:11:41.195 --> 00:11:43.230
there's still a
decent bit of oil

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:48.430
being used for a variety
of energy purposes.

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:53.110
So with oil and all the
other things comes emissions.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:57.150
So what do we think
is the emissions?

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.960
This is getting again
even harder to guess.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:04.230
Metric tons per capita of
carbon dioxide emissions-- so

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:05.970
how many metric
tons do you think

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.640
you are responsible
for each year,

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:10.260
assuming you're the
average American?

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:20.890
1,000, 10,000, 100,000, a
million, a billion, a trillion?

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:21.640
AUDIENCE: 100,000.

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:24.420
AMY BANZAERT: 100,000?

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:24.920
Yeah.

00:12:31.542 --> 00:12:32.810
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:33.810
AMY BANZAERT: I'm sorry?

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:34.930
I heard a number.

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:35.750
AUDIENCE: 1,000.

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:37.440
AMY BANZAERT: 1,000, all right.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:39.930
Anyone want to go above 100k?

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:43.890
OK, and then what about the low?

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:46.740
That should be relatively easy
because it should scale, right?

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:50.760
So if we know that,
on the previous graph,

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:55.830
10,000 was that high energy,
and 100 was the low energy,

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.590
then we should be two orders
of magnitude smaller, right?

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.090
So let's see if that works.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:04.890
But that means we'll
be between 1k and--

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.020
1,000 minus two 0's is 10.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:08.310
Yeah.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:11.910
So who wants to guess
where the high and low is?

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:12.510
Where?

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:13.350
What countries?

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:16.314
AUDIENCE: USA is highest.

00:13:16.314 --> 00:13:17.796
[INAUDIBLE]

00:13:17.796 --> 00:13:18.965
AUDIENCE: China [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:18.965 --> 00:13:20.090
AMY BANZAERT: China's high.

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:23.410
Low?

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:28.832
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:28.832 --> 00:13:29.540
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:35.340
So Congo is indeed low.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:38.190
It doesn't even register.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:43.050
And metric tons is a
big number, so you guys

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:44.400
are nice and high on that.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:48.510
[LAUGHS] We're talking about
10 rather than a zillion.

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:53.220
And the UAE again is
winning over the USA.

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:57.210
And China was not
competitive yet.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.840
I don't know that
they are yet either.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:03.480
OK, so electric
power consumption.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:04.620
Let's do this one fast.

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:10.030
So kilowatt hours-- do
you guys have any sense

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:11.560
of what a kilowatt hour is?

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:17.455
Can anyone give a
physical example?

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:24.560
AUDIENCE: It's a bit like the
amount of power required to run

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:27.000
a light bulb for an hour.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:29.610
AMY BANZAERT: So
a kilowatt hour--

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:33.830
so thinking about light
bulbs is a great approach.

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:39.350
So kilowatt hour-- so, yes, so
definitely how much in an hour.

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:41.900
Do how many watts a
typical light bulb is?

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:42.680
AUDIENCE: 60.

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:43.640
AMY BANZAERT: 60.

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:47.900
So it wouldn't be one
light bulb, right?

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:53.540
It would be 60 times 10 to
get to 600 to get times 2

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:56.430
basically to get
to kilowatt hours.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:15:03.980
So that would be 20
light bulbs in an hour.

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:06.050
So how many--

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:06.950
AUDIENCE: Per person?

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:08.090
AMY BANZAERT: Per person.

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:09.200
Or is it per person?

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:13.880
Yeah, how many
orders-- there's 20.

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:16.940
How many sets of 20
light bulbs are we

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:18.365
powering each year per person?

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:24.820
AUDIENCE: 1,000.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:26.758
AMY BANZAERT: 1,000?

00:15:26.758 --> 00:15:28.606
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] 100,000.

00:15:28.606 --> 00:15:39.272
AMY BANZAERT: 100,000 And low?

00:15:39.272 --> 00:15:40.630
AUDIENCE: 50.

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:43.300
AMY BANZAERT: 50?

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.460
OK, so I'm going to stop
worrying about countries

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:48.190
because it's getting a
little bit repetitive.

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:52.600
So in between, you
guys were spot on.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:58.120
[LAUGHS] And here, the USA
does come ahead of the UAE,

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:01.520
naturally because the UAE is
loaded with oil, and we're not.

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:06.040
So we do use more coal generally
and a little bit of nuclear

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:09.340
to fire our plants,
but mostly coal.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.700
And you can see that, in
this case, Haiti is very low

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:14.410
down, even compared
to the Congo,

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:17.440
in terms of how much
electricity they're using.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:23.260
And you can infer how much
electricity there's access to.

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:25.880
Mobile cellular subscriptions.

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:29.200
So this is no longer
getting quite so ridiculous

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:31.840
in terms of numbers
that we can't imagine.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:36.162
So on the high end, how many
mobile subscriptions per person

00:16:36.162 --> 00:16:37.120
do you think there are?

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:38.920
AUDIENCE: Is this a
percentage [INAUDIBLE]??

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:40.270
AMY BANZAERT: So per 100 people.

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:41.252
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:41.252 --> 00:16:42.085
AMY BANZAERT: So 80.

00:16:46.306 --> 00:16:48.766
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:48.766 --> 00:16:50.734
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:50.734 --> 00:16:52.561
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:16:52.561 --> 00:16:53.686
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it's just--

00:16:53.686 --> 00:16:55.680
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.710
AMY BANZAERT: So this
is the population.

00:16:58.710 --> 00:17:01.530
I assume they go with like--
they don't go to infants.

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:03.270
I assume they go to
starting with people

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:05.579
who might have a cell
phone, but I actually

00:17:05.579 --> 00:17:06.571
don't know that answer.

00:17:06.571 --> 00:17:07.529
That's a good question.

00:17:07.529 --> 00:17:10.124
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:17:10.124 --> 00:17:11.249
AMY BANZAERT: Like 90, 100?

00:17:11.249 --> 00:17:12.160
AUDIENCE: 90.

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:14.771
AMY BANZAERT: OK, and
who do you think wins?

00:17:14.771 --> 00:17:15.646
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:15.646 --> 00:17:16.188
AUDIENCE: US.

00:17:16.188 --> 00:17:17.380
AUDIENCE: China.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:20.893
AMY BANZAERT: US,
China anyone else?

00:17:20.893 --> 00:17:21.868
AUDIENCE: Congo.

00:17:21.868 --> 00:17:22.660
AMY BANZAERT: Wins?

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:23.792
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:23.792 --> 00:17:24.500
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:33.143
And then on the
low end, how many?

00:17:33.143 --> 00:17:34.472
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] 30.

00:17:34.472 --> 00:17:35.180
AMY BANZAERT: 30?

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:37.318
Anyone want to go lower
or higher on the low end?

00:17:37.318 --> 00:17:37.860
AUDIENCE: 10.

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:38.402
AUDIENCE: 10.

00:17:38.402 --> 00:17:40.220
AMY BANZAERT: 10, 5.

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:42.350
OK, and who do you
think is on the low end?

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:43.070
AUDIENCE: Haiti.

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:46.810
AMY BANZAERT: Haiti, who else?

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:47.310
OK.

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:52.810
So this one's
pretty interesting,

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:54.030
and this is year to year.

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:58.200
So in 2008, there were more than
two cell phone subscriptions

00:17:58.200 --> 00:17:59.430
per person in the UAE.

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:00.180
AUDIENCE: Awesome.

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:04.740
AMY BANZAERT: [LAUGHS] So
they like their phones there.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:08.502
In the US, we're not
quite at 100 yet.

00:18:08.502 --> 00:18:09.960
But we're getting
close to everyone

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:11.760
having a cell phone on average.

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:14.100
And there is that "some
people have more than one

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.000
phone" issue.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:19.410
But you can see
that, in every place,

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.475
there's a pretty
strong growth curve.

00:18:21.475 --> 00:18:23.100
And that's why the
cell phone companies

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.830
are raking in the bucks
right now worldwide.

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:28.240
Like, Haiti going from 1 to 34--

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:29.730
that's a pretty
phenomenal growth.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:33.847
And everyone, even the Republic
of Congo, is doing pretty well.

00:18:33.847 --> 00:18:35.430
Though, thinking
they were at the top,

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:38.550
I'm afraid is not the case.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:41.022
But certainly the growth
rate in developing countries

00:18:41.022 --> 00:18:43.230
is really high because people
are getting cell phones

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:48.910
before they get a lot of
other more developed devices.

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:50.820
And then lastly, internet users.

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:57.796
so what's your best guess there?

00:18:57.796 --> 00:18:58.357
AUDIENCE: 90.

00:18:58.357 --> 00:18:59.065
AMY BANZAERT: 90?

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:05.560
US, UAE?

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:07.243
AUDIENCE: Does
this include access

00:19:07.243 --> 00:19:10.515
to the internet [INAUDIBLE]?

00:19:10.515 --> 00:19:11.640
AMY BANZAERT: I don't know.

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:15.120
I think this study was probably
done before that was quite as

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:15.810
prevalent.

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:20.520
Because even in 2008,
you were using your phone

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:25.020
for internet access a whole lot
less than you're doing in 2011.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:27.070
But let's say-- I
actually don't know.

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:29.460
So I can't even say,
"let's say something."

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:31.540
But, yeah, I'm not
grading you on this,

00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:33.007
so it doesn't really matter.

00:19:33.007 --> 00:19:34.090
It's just to give a sense.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:39.700
So what-- 90, does anyone
want to take a different?

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:41.764
And then on the low end?

00:19:41.764 --> 00:19:42.306
AUDIENCE: 20.

00:19:42.306 --> 00:19:43.290
AUDIENCE: 1.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:46.330
AMY BANZAERT: 20, 1.

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:47.620
OK.

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:52.810
So here, you can see that we're
pretty far behind in terms

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:56.080
of internet versus
cell phones, which

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:59.090
isn't all that surprising.

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:02.380
So the US is lower, the
UAE is a whole lot lower.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:03.760
And in the developing
countries--

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.040
like Republic of Congo,
you might remember,

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:09.850
I think they were at 14 people
had cell phones per 100.

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:11.890
And they're at half
a person per 100.

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:14.500
So internet access
is a whole lot lower.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:16.750
And you can think about
the reasons for that,

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:20.020
and they probably make
sense why it's harder

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:22.720
to get internet access than cell
phone access in a developing

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:24.230
country.

00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:27.070
So that was our ridiculous quiz.

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:31.030
What might you take away
from trying to guess

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:32.698
those ridiculous things?

00:20:32.698 --> 00:20:34.450
AUDIENCE: We need
more D-Lab classes.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:36.250
AMY BANZAERT: You need more
D-Lab classes, absolutely.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:37.000
I'll support that.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:37.570
What else?

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:42.080
Yeah?

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:43.580
AUDIENCE: Developed
countries really

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:45.890
contribute to global issues.

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:48.165
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, developed
countries absolutely do.

00:20:48.165 --> 00:20:49.790
I don't think that'll
come as a shocker

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:52.610
to anyone who's read the
newspaper in the past five

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:53.370
years.

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:55.610
But, yeah, but it's helpful
to see the actual numbers

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:58.490
as opposed to just a
gut sense, totally.

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:00.284
What else?

00:21:00.284 --> 00:21:01.268
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:21:01.268 --> 00:21:02.867
AUDIENCE:
--technologies, like when

00:21:02.867 --> 00:21:04.284
you have cell
phones [INAUDIBLE],,

00:21:04.284 --> 00:21:07.680
there's a possibility of
something else [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:10.820
embrace it and use
it for two years.

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:12.488
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
so a technology

00:21:12.488 --> 00:21:14.030
that actually really
works for people

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:16.580
is going to be
embraced, absolutely.

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:21.290
And one like an
internet workstation

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:25.220
may not be one that is
embraced because it's

00:21:25.220 --> 00:21:26.550
impossible to embrace.

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:28.213
So that's a good takeaway too.

00:21:28.213 --> 00:21:29.630
One thing I take
away from this is

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:32.600
that there's not much
gut instinct here for me

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:37.700
either that we talk about
metric tons of carbon dioxide

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:38.210
emissions.

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:40.502
We talked about all these
different things in the news.

00:21:40.502 --> 00:21:44.330
But do we actually, as MIT
scientists and engineers, who

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:47.060
are some of the brightest people
in the world on these sorts

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.100
of topics, know these details?

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:49.790
Heck no.

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:51.890
So when you're starting
to think about what

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:54.470
it means to do this work
in a developing country,

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:57.260
making assumptions like how
many cell phone users there are,

00:21:57.260 --> 00:21:59.270
or how many internet
users there are,

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:02.000
or any other detail about life
is a pretty dangerous thing

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:03.050
to make an assumption on.

00:22:03.050 --> 00:22:08.630
And you have to get the data
in order to do good work.

00:22:08.630 --> 00:22:11.850
There are some trends with
poverty that we can take away.

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:16.640
So you did see
Nicaragua, the Congo,

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:18.387
and Haiti were always
on the lower end

00:22:18.387 --> 00:22:20.720
compared to the more developed
countries are looking at.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:23.750
But Haiti, Nicaragua,
and the Congo all

00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:25.460
took turns who was the lowest.

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:28.340
And so even though you
can very distinctly

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:31.663
say who is making the
least income per capita,

00:22:31.663 --> 00:22:33.080
that doesn't
necessarily translate

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:35.455
to who has the fewest cell
phone subscribers or something

00:22:35.455 --> 00:22:37.140
like that.

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:41.260
And the last thing
is when you watch--

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:43.136
yeah, do you have a question?

00:22:43.136 --> 00:22:44.642
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:44.642 --> 00:22:46.350
But I was just going
to say that takeaway

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:49.360
was the opportunity that these
countries are developing,

00:22:49.360 --> 00:22:49.860
though.

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:52.100
And so we're enabling
them to [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:53.690
and while that's
another thing, it'd

00:22:53.690 --> 00:22:56.185
be great if they never
developed [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:56.185 --> 00:22:57.560
AMY BANZAERT: That
sure would be,

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:00.890
and that's one of the challenges
for doing this type of work

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:05.090
is everyone pretty much
wants, hypothetically,

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:08.030
to lead a life where
everyone has a car,

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:10.310
and everyone has air
conditioning running or heat

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:12.080
running 24/7 like we do.

00:23:12.080 --> 00:23:16.760
And if we try to create
our environment worldwide,

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:20.360
we're not going to have
a world for very long.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:24.200
One other takeaway I have is
that you can get an assumption

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:26.840
if you've never traveled to
developing countries when you

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:30.200
see the photos of charity
for commercials and stuff

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:32.300
like that of how hard life is.

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:34.640
You can assume that
life is completely dire

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:37.820
and that 0% of people have
access to clean water,

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:40.610
0% have access to electricity,
no one has a cell phone,

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:41.880
and that's not true either.

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:45.230
And so you neither want to
assume that everyone has this,

00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:47.690
but you don't want to assume
that life is impossible,

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:51.560
and everyone's living in the
Dilbert mud universe, where

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:55.522
all you have is mud, and that's
it, which is easy to happen

00:23:55.522 --> 00:23:57.230
if you've never
traveled, and all you see

00:23:57.230 --> 00:23:58.967
are Save the Children ads on TV.

00:23:58.967 --> 00:24:01.550
Not to bash Save the Children,
but their ads are pretty stark.

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:05.930
[LAUGHS] This is tough.

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:11.030
OK, one other way to
think about development

00:24:11.030 --> 00:24:13.670
is just to look at
the world at night,

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:16.340
which NASA does by
compiling a bunch of photos

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:17.960
of pictures at night.

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:21.800
And so you can very quickly
see where lights are

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:24.950
and where the wealth is, the
drawback being that fire also

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:28.310
creates light, and so areas
where there are big fires

00:24:28.310 --> 00:24:30.900
will also look like
they're well developed.

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:33.290
And so that's often the case
that, in Africa, there's

00:24:33.290 --> 00:24:35.750
fires burning out of
control, and that's

00:24:35.750 --> 00:24:38.810
why it looks like
it's developed.

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:40.460
But just a kitchen
fire is not going

00:24:40.460 --> 00:24:42.890
to show up on a NASA
graph, but it's an easy way

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:46.310
to think about where our
population centers in developed

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:50.780
countries and then also
where is enough money to have

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:52.010
electricity, to have lights.

00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:58.100
So you can see things like
India is actually pretty lit,

00:24:58.100 --> 00:25:01.070
and that plays out in terms
of how India is doing.

00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:03.590
Its really been skyrocketing
in terms of its development,

00:25:03.590 --> 00:25:04.880
and so there's a
lot more electricity

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:06.170
there than there was,
though there's certainly

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:08.300
plenty of work to be
done in that country

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:10.080
to make life easier.

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:11.310
Was there a question?

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:11.810
Yeah.

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:13.260
AUDIENCE: I was
going to say, does

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:16.136
the map like that [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:16.136 --> 00:25:17.480
And it kind of looks the same.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:17.956
AMY BANZAERT: [LAUGHS]

00:25:17.956 --> 00:25:19.498
AUDIENCE: And somehow
you have this--

00:25:19.498 --> 00:25:21.230
like even China right
now is pretty dark,

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:23.128
which is quite surprising
'cause you expect to China to be

00:25:23.128 --> 00:25:23.730
more like [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:24.815
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, yeah.

00:25:24.815 --> 00:25:26.110
AUDIENCE: The same with
Facebook, actually.

00:25:26.110 --> 00:25:26.610
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:26.880
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:28.922
AMY BANZAERT: Well, and
it's kind of fascinating.

00:25:28.922 --> 00:25:30.600
I think in every
developing country,

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:32.880
there's not an even
distribution of wealth.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:35.100
Just like in this country,
you've got your Bill Gates

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:36.960
and Warren Buffetts,
and you have

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:39.570
people who are starving
to death in this country.

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:41.370
You have that true
in every country.

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:45.810
In Haiti, you can see mansions
and you can see hovels.

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:47.740
And you can see that everywhere.

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:50.850
And so there's always going
to be that uneven distribution

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:54.280
wherever you go.

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:56.700
So Nicaragua-- why
did I pick Nicaragua

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.020
as the place to travel?

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:02.790
Well, it's a pretty great place
in terms of renewable energy

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:04.780
sources.

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:10.360
So you can see that it has
a lot of different sources.

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:11.930
It has wind on the coast.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:14.430
It has a bunch of volcanoes,
which provide geothermal energy

00:26:14.430 --> 00:26:16.080
that they're taking
advantage of,

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:18.300
rivers that can
provide hydropower,

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:21.120
solar energy because it's
basically on the equator,

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:23.940
and they grow sugarcane,
so you can grow ethanol.

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:27.150
So it's this really
nice place in terms

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:28.800
of lots of opportunity,
as compared

00:26:28.800 --> 00:26:30.342
to some other
countries where there's

00:26:30.342 --> 00:26:31.360
fewer natural resources.

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:33.612
And so if you want to
start in a country,

00:26:33.612 --> 00:26:35.070
it's nice to start
in one where you

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:36.373
have something to start with.

00:26:36.373 --> 00:26:38.790
But Nicaragua is the second
poorest country in the Western

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:40.080
Hemisphere behind Haiti.

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:43.410
And, yes, Haiti is well
behind Nicaragua, and more so

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:44.430
after the earthquake.

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:46.725
But Nicaragua still
has a lot of need.

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:53.980
I forget exactly when, but about
10 or 15 years ago Nicaragua

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:57.040
was about 70% renewable energy.

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:59.440
And as people's desire
for energy has gone up,

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:01.450
and the cost of
oil has stabilized,

00:27:01.450 --> 00:27:04.360
they've actually sunk
back down to about 30%

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:07.780
renewable energy, which is a sad
thing-- especially considering

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:11.390
that Costa Rica, I think, is
about 90% or 95% renewable

00:27:11.390 --> 00:27:11.890
energy.

00:27:11.890 --> 00:27:12.680
So it can be done.

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:16.120
Costa Rica is a next-door
neighbor to Nicaragua.

00:27:16.120 --> 00:27:20.500
When their goal is, by 2013,
which is getting ever closer,

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:23.200
they want to be at 3% oil,
which is pretty ambitious, given

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:26.230
that they're at
about 70% right now.

00:27:26.230 --> 00:27:29.320
But that translates to there's
a lot of interest in Nicaragua

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:32.650
to use renewables.

00:27:32.650 --> 00:27:37.030
So that makes doing work in
that country easier as well.

00:27:37.030 --> 00:27:39.070
They're also close
by because we're

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:42.220
traveling over spring break,
and that's a very fast trip.

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:43.990
The idea of flying
all the way to Africa

00:27:43.990 --> 00:27:45.628
and spending half
your time in the air

00:27:45.628 --> 00:27:47.920
and spending all of your
budget on the airplane tickets

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:49.878
is not a great idea, so
that's the other reason

00:27:49.878 --> 00:27:52.360
Nicaragua is of interest.

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:55.795
OK, so let's talk a
little bit about energy.

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:04.780
So we talked a little
bit about how much

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:06.170
energy is used worldwide.

00:28:06.170 --> 00:28:09.050
So let's talk about
where it's coming from.

00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:13.900
And you can see that coal
and oil really dominate where

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:15.730
our energy is coming from.

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:18.550
And all these renewable
energies that we talk about

00:28:18.550 --> 00:28:21.070
are pretty microscopic.

00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:28.720
So the world has a long way
to go before we can actually

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:31.540
reduce our greenhouse gas
emissions and things like that.

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:35.380
There's a really big
problem with this slide.

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:38.852
Does anyone see it?

00:28:38.852 --> 00:28:39.352
[LAUGHS]

00:28:44.327 --> 00:28:45.910
AMY BANZAERT: Email
me if you find it.

00:28:45.910 --> 00:28:49.780
[LAUGHS] I'll give
you extra credit.

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:51.250
AUDIENCE: Doesn't add up?

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:52.250
AMY BANZAERT: Say again?

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:53.500
AUDIENCE: Does it not add up?

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:54.708
AMY BANZAERT: It does add up.

00:28:54.708 --> 00:28:57.106
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:57.106 --> 00:28:59.511
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:29:00.192 --> 00:29:00.900
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:03.540
So and now, exergy and--

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:05.730
for the purposes
of right now, you

00:29:05.730 --> 00:29:09.058
can consider exergy and energy
to be basically the same thing

00:29:09.058 --> 00:29:09.600
in your head.

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:10.987
That's a total lie.

00:29:10.987 --> 00:29:12.570
And if you know
anything about exergy,

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:14.880
you know I'm lying
to you, but it's not

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:17.740
that critical to think
about the difference.

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:20.760
And so you can see where
are we spending our energy.

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:25.230
And it's actually
pretty well distributed

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:29.250
between all the different
needs, not entirely.

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:34.440
Others are carefully constructed
to be about the same size.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:38.940
But what this means
is we can't just

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:41.580
somehow magically replace
all the cars in the world

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.110
and solve all our
energy problems

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:46.950
or somehow magically
stop needing agriculture

00:29:46.950 --> 00:29:50.410
and have no more greenhouse
gas emission problems.

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:52.765
There's a lot of
issues worldwide

00:29:52.765 --> 00:29:54.390
that need to be
addressed, and they all

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:55.640
need to be taken into account.

00:30:01.270 --> 00:30:04.830
OK, so and why is it
so hard to switch?

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:08.470
Well, the energy density of
what we're used to using--

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:11.280
so diesel and
gasoline and such--

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:16.980
are awesome compared to
batteries, flywheels,

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:19.020
compressed air,
some of the things

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:22.260
that we talk about as being
the great alternatives.

00:30:22.260 --> 00:30:26.280
And so going from something
that's an order of magnitude

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:30.950
worse and is harder to
come by is not an easy sell

00:30:30.950 --> 00:30:33.450
because it means it's going to
be a whole lot more expensive

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:34.750
and less pleasant to use.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:37.510
So that's why this is
such a difficult problem.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:40.110
But because we're running out
of those really awesome ones,

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:42.720
we're going to have
to figure it out.

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:44.350
OK, so units check-in.

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:46.920
So one of your sheet says
"Units Check-in" on it.

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:49.410
And so you can pair up
preferably with someone

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:53.070
you don't know and
go through the units.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:54.840
Again, I'm not going
to grade you on this,

00:30:54.840 --> 00:30:57.810
but I would like to have you
turn it in with your name

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:04.800
so I know where the class stands
overall on this knowledge.

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:08.010
And you guys can work in teams
and just turn one sheet in.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:09.862
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:31:14.030 --> 00:31:17.990
AMY BANZAERT: This is an area
