00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:36.890
I wasn't trying to slam Harvard.

00:31:36.890 --> 00:31:40.880
But these are
actually things that--

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:44.570
there's a few in here that are a
little subtle, like inductance,

00:31:44.570 --> 00:31:46.820
that you're not going
to need to know.

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:49.980
That was just to check
how everyone is doing.

00:31:49.980 --> 00:31:55.220
But most of them-- torque,
work, power, energy, pressure,

00:31:55.220 --> 00:31:56.390
resistance--

00:31:56.390 --> 00:31:57.890
those, you should know.

00:31:57.890 --> 00:31:59.837
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:59.837 --> 00:32:00.920
AMY BANZAERT: Are you out?

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:02.088
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:32:02.088 --> 00:32:03.755
AUDIENCE: I think we
need more because--

00:32:03.755 --> 00:32:04.904
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:32:04.904 --> 00:32:05.612
AMY BANZAERT: Oh.

00:32:05.612 --> 00:32:06.237
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:32:06.237 --> 00:32:07.430
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:09.560
AMY BANZAERT: So
those are things

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:13.430
that are all really useful to
know for the rest of your life.

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:16.850
It's really handy to know
how energy and power relate,

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:19.820
what is energy, what is power,
and something that most people

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:22.220
don't know, including
engineers and scientists,

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:25.610
and is an embarrassment to our
educational system that most

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:26.270
of us don't.

00:32:26.270 --> 00:32:28.480
So I hope that,
through this class,

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:30.980
you will be forced to get it
into your brain for long enough

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:33.320
that it'll stick.

00:32:33.320 --> 00:32:37.440
So, yeah, definitely
learn that stuff.

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:39.320
All right, so what is energy?

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:41.817
Anyone?

00:32:41.817 --> 00:32:43.400
You've got your units
in front of you.

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:44.192
Maybe that'll help.

00:32:49.783 --> 00:32:51.553
AUDIENCE: A measure
of potential work.

00:32:51.553 --> 00:32:53.220
AMY BANZAERT: Measure
of potential work.

00:32:56.820 --> 00:33:01.235
So then what's the difference
between work and energy?

00:33:01.235 --> 00:33:04.130
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:06.250
AMY BANZAERT: So energy
is the ability to do work.

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:11.150
Anyone else?

00:33:16.250 --> 00:33:19.927
The measure of potential
work, did you say?

00:33:19.927 --> 00:33:20.552
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:33:20.552 --> 00:33:21.260
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:29.300
And then what's work, then?

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:36.578
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:36.578 --> 00:33:38.620
AMY BANZAERT: I can't hear
you back there, sorry.

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:41.043
AUDIENCE: Force over
distance [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:41.043 --> 00:33:42.460
AMY BANZAERT: Force
over distance.

00:33:42.460 --> 00:33:47.410
OK, can you give me something
that's a little more intuitive?

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:50.950
Because I don't have
a great intuition

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:52.840
of force over distance,
though, kind of I do.

00:33:52.840 --> 00:33:54.090
I think I could work that out.

00:33:54.090 --> 00:34:01.803
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] you're
required to [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:01.803 --> 00:34:02.970
over a distance [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:03.928
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, OK.

00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:19.110
So the thing that confuses me
about that is you said, work

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:20.969
is force over distance.

00:34:20.969 --> 00:34:23.070
And then you said,
it's the energy

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:24.800
to move an object
over a given distance.

00:34:24.800 --> 00:34:26.508
So then I would think
the equation should

00:34:26.508 --> 00:34:27.929
be E over d, not F over d.

00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:28.762
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:34:28.762 --> 00:34:31.261
AUDIENCE: --force applied over
a distance [INAUDIBLE] force

00:34:31.261 --> 00:34:32.917
into [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:32.917 --> 00:34:33.750
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:34:33.750 --> 00:34:35.940
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:38.594
AMY BANZAERT: OK, so
force over a distance.

00:34:38.594 --> 00:34:41.730
[LAUGHS] OK, cool.

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:43.139
Any other definitions?

00:34:48.909 --> 00:34:52.840
OK, so the way
that Saul Griffith

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:55.340
likes to think about it-- and
I took these slides from him.

00:34:55.340 --> 00:34:58.270
He's an MIT alum who does a
lot of work in energy realms,

00:34:58.270 --> 00:35:00.220
as well as design and
other great stuff--

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:05.710
is it's thinking
about lifting an apple

00:35:05.710 --> 00:35:08.390
from the ground to a table.

00:35:08.390 --> 00:35:11.188
So does anyone know how
much an apple weighs?

00:35:11.188 --> 00:35:13.060
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:13.060 --> 00:35:14.050
AMY BANZAERT: A newton?

00:35:14.050 --> 00:35:15.490
Yeah, that's a
really good guess.

00:35:15.490 --> 00:35:17.140
Does anyone know how
much a newton is?

00:35:17.140 --> 00:35:22.480
[LAUGHS] So, well--

00:35:22.480 --> 00:35:23.845
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:27.253 --> 00:35:29.170
AMY BANZAERT: Well, so
a kilogram and a newton

00:35:29.170 --> 00:35:30.940
relate, and how do they relate?

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:32.320
AUDIENCE: 1 kilogram per newton.

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:33.850
AMY BANZAERT:
Yeah, so we're just

00:35:33.850 --> 00:35:36.250
talking about mass
versus weight, right?

00:35:36.250 --> 00:35:40.450
So mass is disregarding
gravity, and weight

00:35:40.450 --> 00:35:41.710
is regarding gravity.

00:35:41.710 --> 00:35:43.690
And so a newton is taking
into account gravity.

00:35:43.690 --> 00:35:45.550
But, yeah, an apple
is about half a pound,

00:35:45.550 --> 00:35:47.020
which is about a newton.

00:35:47.020 --> 00:35:50.080
So I have two
apples here, so you

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:53.410
can get a physical intuition
for that because physical--

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:56.050
so just pass them around.

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:58.630
Physical intuition is
really, really handy.

00:35:58.630 --> 00:36:02.770
And I know the
lengths of my thumb

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:05.590
so that I know how long an
inches and how long 7 inches is

00:36:05.590 --> 00:36:08.750
and hopefully have some sense
of weights and distances too.

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:11.240
So that's why it's really
nice to have an equivalent.

00:36:11.240 --> 00:36:14.980
And you can even take that apple
and feel what it physically

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:20.440
feels like to lift that apple
from the ground to a table.

00:36:20.440 --> 00:36:22.090
And because a table
is about a meter--

00:36:22.090 --> 00:36:24.520
so we're talking about
a newton-meter, which

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:27.580
is your joule.

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:29.785
So then what is power?

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:35.560
AUDIENCE: Energy
for a given time.

00:36:35.560 --> 00:36:37.020
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, exactly.

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:40.440
So we could think
about how many--

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:44.160
the watts associated with
moving a bunch of apples

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:45.610
from the ground to a table.

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:48.450
So moving 40 apples from
the ground to a table

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:54.160
is 40 watts, which is the power
associated with a light bulb.

00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:57.330
And so light bulbs are
using a good bit of work

00:36:57.330 --> 00:37:01.530
if you think about
how much work it

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:05.595
takes to move 40 apples from
the ground to the table.

00:37:05.595 --> 00:37:07.530
You can do it, but
you wouldn't want

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:11.022
to do it all day every day for
as long as your lights are on.

00:37:11.022 --> 00:37:12.390
AUDIENCE: Also [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:14.348
AMY BANZAERT: Right,
right, for a second, yeah.

00:37:14.348 --> 00:37:15.960
So you're doing
that every second

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:19.200
if you're powering your
light bulb hypothetically.

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:22.260
So does that make
sense to everyone?

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:25.260
If this is a good
analogy for you, awesome.

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:25.830
Use it.

00:37:25.830 --> 00:37:26.920
Keep it in your head.

00:37:26.920 --> 00:37:29.670
If it's a terrible analogy
for you, which it probably

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:32.520
is for some of you,
get creative and think

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:35.040
about what might be a
good analogy for you.

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:36.340
You can google on the web.

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:37.800
There's lots of different
people who explain

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:39.092
this in lots of different ways.

00:37:39.092 --> 00:37:41.580
But these are two concepts
that is really good

00:37:41.580 --> 00:37:43.590
to have in your mind.

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.830
The other one that
I like to do, which

00:37:46.830 --> 00:37:51.390
is a little obscure in some
respects but really helps me,

00:37:51.390 --> 00:38:02.700
is to recall that power is
analogous to a velocity,

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:09.700
and energy is analogous
to a distance.

00:38:09.700 --> 00:38:13.140
So that's another way to think
of it in addition to the apple

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:14.430
analogy.

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:18.250
And however you're
thinking about it--

00:38:18.250 --> 00:38:21.070
so running that Apple laptop--

00:38:21.070 --> 00:38:24.900
I don't know what
this HP or IBM takes.

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:27.880
But it takes about 60 watts.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:30.960
So it's moving 60
of those apples

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:36.047
every second for as
long as I keep it on.

00:38:36.047 --> 00:38:38.130
The other way to think
about in addition to apples

00:38:38.130 --> 00:38:38.820
is light bulbs.

00:38:38.820 --> 00:38:41.010
Because, again, we have
light bulbs as a quantity

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:46.140
that we kind of know, and we
know that powering a light bulb

00:38:46.140 --> 00:38:48.600
is a whole lot easier
than powering a toaster

00:38:48.600 --> 00:38:50.040
oven or a space heater.

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:52.600
So you can kind of
think in those terms.

00:38:52.600 --> 00:38:57.480
So when someone says something
is a kilowatt generator,

00:38:57.480 --> 00:38:59.160
you can think of
what we did before.

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:01.550
You can think about how many
light bulbs in a kilowatt.

00:39:01.550 --> 00:39:02.050
Yeah?

00:39:02.050 --> 00:39:03.870
AUDIENCE: Would a
light bulb [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:07.440
is really more powerful.

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:11.070
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so
energy and power are--

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:13.890
the difference is whether you're
thinking about time or not.

00:39:13.890 --> 00:39:17.070
So energy, if you think about
it in terms of distance,

00:39:17.070 --> 00:39:19.500
I can say that I
walked a mile today.

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:20.670
And you can say, great.

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:23.760
And if I did it in four
minutes, you can say, wow.

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:25.200
And if I did it
in four hours, you

00:39:25.200 --> 00:39:27.960
can say, unimpressive, right?

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:30.510
And so energy is
just telling you

00:39:30.510 --> 00:39:33.930
that the equivalent of
how many miles you walked,

00:39:33.930 --> 00:39:36.390
and power is telling you
how quickly you did it.

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:39.850
Both are really important.

00:39:39.850 --> 00:39:43.440
So a light bulb that's
40 watts is telling you

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:46.530
how much energy is
going to be required

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:51.150
in a given amount of time to
turn it on, to keep it on.

00:39:51.150 --> 00:39:52.962
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:52.962 --> 00:39:54.170
AMY BANZAERT: It's confusing.

00:39:54.170 --> 00:39:56.330
I promise you I will
screw up and say

00:39:56.330 --> 00:39:58.490
the wrong word occasionally.

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:01.490
And if you do, and you catch
me, I really appreciate it.

00:40:01.490 --> 00:40:04.310
So all right, so let's do--

00:40:04.310 --> 00:40:09.088
oh, sorry, are there any
questions about all of this?

00:40:09.088 --> 00:40:10.130
It's hard to think about.

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:13.940
I've been in grad classes where
students screw it up royally.

00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:16.880
So don't be saddened if you do.

00:40:16.880 --> 00:40:20.180
But it is really important
to get a good intuition on it

00:40:20.180 --> 00:40:22.250
in any energy class,
and hopefully,

00:40:22.250 --> 00:40:24.470
as every engineer
or scientist will

00:40:24.470 --> 00:40:28.700
have one by the end
of their career here.

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:30.108
Any other questions?

00:40:33.250 --> 00:40:38.762
OK, so hopefully this equation
looks familiar to everyone.

00:40:38.762 --> 00:40:39.970
Is there anyone who doesn't--

00:40:39.970 --> 00:40:41.678
I know this is awful
to ask, but is there

00:40:41.678 --> 00:40:44.680
anyone this doesn't
look familiar to?

00:40:44.680 --> 00:40:46.055
Because this deals
with something

00:40:46.055 --> 00:40:47.805
that is an assignment
that's due tomorrow.

00:40:47.805 --> 00:40:49.210
So if it doesn't
look familiar, I

00:40:49.210 --> 00:40:50.710
need to give you a different
assignment, because that's not

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:51.910
fair.

00:40:51.910 --> 00:40:53.590
So if it doesn't look
familiar, and you

00:40:53.590 --> 00:40:55.480
don't want to embarrass
yourself right now--

00:40:55.480 --> 00:40:57.910
or not embarrass yourself,
but be open about it--

00:40:57.910 --> 00:41:00.830
let me know after class.

00:41:00.830 --> 00:41:03.070
So what's the V?

00:41:03.070 --> 00:41:04.420
AUDIENCE: Voltage.

00:41:04.420 --> 00:41:06.562
AMY BANZAERT: And
what's voltage?

00:41:06.562 --> 00:41:07.770
AUDIENCE: Joules per coulomb.

00:41:10.265 --> 00:41:11.890
AMY BANZAERT: I
couldn't hear all that.

00:41:11.890 --> 00:41:17.380
So voltage is what--
is joules per coulomb?

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:18.955
Anything else?

00:41:18.955 --> 00:41:20.330
AUDIENCE: Difference
in potential

00:41:20.330 --> 00:41:21.508
between the two terminals.

00:41:21.508 --> 00:41:22.300
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:41:22.300 --> 00:41:23.920
The difference in potential.

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:29.710
I apologize about
my handwriting.

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:43.195
And can you think of the
analogy, the classic analogy

00:41:43.195 --> 00:41:43.695
for voltage?

00:41:46.986 --> 00:41:48.460
AUDIENCE: Water pipe stuff.

00:41:48.460 --> 00:41:50.470
AMY BANZAERT: You said,
water pipe stuff, yeah.

00:41:50.470 --> 00:41:52.120
So what's voltage?

00:41:52.120 --> 00:41:53.818
AUDIENCE: It's
the height for it.

00:41:53.818 --> 00:41:54.610
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:41:54.610 --> 00:41:56.935
So the water-- the height,
the water pressure.

00:42:02.190 --> 00:42:03.690
All right, how about I?

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:08.290
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] current.

00:42:08.290 --> 00:42:11.840
AMY BANZAERT: Current, yep.

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:13.400
And current is?

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:15.428
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] amps.

00:42:15.428 --> 00:42:16.220
AMY BANZAERT: Amps.

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:18.800
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] flow.

00:42:18.800 --> 00:42:21.260
AMY BANZAERT: And
the flow, yeah.

00:42:21.260 --> 00:42:22.657
And that analogy is easy.

00:42:22.657 --> 00:42:23.990
You can think of it as the flow.

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:25.634
And then what's R?

00:42:25.634 --> 00:42:27.290
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:30.292
AMY BANZAERT: And
so that would be?

00:42:30.292 --> 00:42:31.250
AUDIENCE: The resistor.

00:42:31.250 --> 00:42:32.208
AMY BANZAERT: The ohms.

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:35.780
Yep, go ahead.

00:42:35.780 --> 00:42:38.660
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
the diameter of the exit.

00:42:38.660 --> 00:42:45.360
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah so the pipe
exit diameter, et cetera, yeah.

00:42:45.360 --> 00:42:47.370
So this is a handy
equation to know.

00:42:47.370 --> 00:42:49.810
We don't have to do too
much EE in this class,

00:42:49.810 --> 00:42:53.040
but you definitely have to do.

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:55.470
Electricity always bugged
me because it's invisible.

00:42:55.470 --> 00:42:57.900
And as a mechanical
engineer, I don't really

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:00.940
enjoy thinking about
things that are invisible.

00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:04.980
So I think it's
tough, and, yeah,

00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:07.080
I like the flow
rate analogy a lot,

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:09.617
thinking about the
pressure and the water flow

00:43:09.617 --> 00:43:10.950
and the resistance to that flow.

00:43:10.950 --> 00:43:12.850
That helps me a lot.

00:43:12.850 --> 00:43:16.200
So why am I reviewing
this to you?

00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:19.080
What is useful about
these quantities?

00:43:26.020 --> 00:43:30.400
So in this class, one is we
can predict battery life.

00:43:30.400 --> 00:43:35.010
So batteries are measured
in amp hours, so how much

00:43:35.010 --> 00:43:36.900
flow over how much time.

00:43:36.900 --> 00:43:38.580
So you can know how
long your battery

00:43:38.580 --> 00:43:40.913
is going to last, which is
really important when you're,

00:43:40.913 --> 00:43:43.200
say, sizing a solar
system, a solar panel

00:43:43.200 --> 00:43:45.430
system, or something like that.

00:43:45.430 --> 00:43:47.460
Secondly, not destroying LEDs--

00:43:47.460 --> 00:43:50.700
so I'm about to give
you an LED and a battery

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:53.100
and ask you to figure
out what size resistor

00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:57.190
you need to connect the two and
then do a couple other things.

00:43:57.190 --> 00:44:00.060
So let's review how to
calculate that really quickly.

00:44:00.060 --> 00:44:01.410
Can anyone walk me through it?

00:44:05.170 --> 00:44:07.060
AUDIENCE: What's the
size of the battery?

00:44:07.060 --> 00:44:10.080
AMY BANZAERT: So the
battery is a 9 volt.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:12.780
AUDIENCE: Do we know which
current that we'd use?

00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:22.020
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah so the LED
is a 20-milliamp-hour LED.

00:44:22.020 --> 00:44:23.960
What else do you need to know?

00:44:23.960 --> 00:44:25.430
AUDIENCE: How long [INAUDIBLE].

00:44:25.430 --> 00:44:26.388
AMY BANZAERT: Hm?

00:44:26.388 --> 00:44:28.728
AUDIENCE: How long [INAUDIBLE].

00:44:28.728 --> 00:44:30.672
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:30.672 --> 00:44:32.630
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so
how long you're powering

00:44:32.630 --> 00:44:33.637
it might be important.

00:44:33.637 --> 00:44:34.970
In this case, it's a flashlight.

00:44:34.970 --> 00:44:36.380
So it's something you're
going to turn on and off,

00:44:36.380 --> 00:44:38.150
and you can probably
estimate how long you're

00:44:38.150 --> 00:44:38.858
going to need it.

00:44:38.858 --> 00:44:40.730
The voltage, you're
actually going to need,

00:44:40.730 --> 00:44:44.300
so it's a 3.6-volt LED.

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:46.040
Does anyone know
what LED stands for?

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:47.600
AUDIENCE: Light-Emitting Diode.

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:49.725
AMY BANZAERT: And does
anyone know what a diode is?

00:44:49.725 --> 00:44:51.062
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:51.062 --> 00:44:52.020
AMY BANZAERT: Go ahead.

00:44:52.020 --> 00:44:57.320
AUDIENCE: It's essentially
a one-way pathway

00:44:57.320 --> 00:45:00.768
in which [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:00.768 --> 00:45:01.560
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:03.120
AUDIENCE: So, essentially,
it's a one-way gate.

00:45:03.120 --> 00:45:05.370
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so it's
absolutely a one-way gate,

00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:07.410
and the light-emitting
diodes happen

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:09.420
to emit light when the
current passes through,

00:45:09.420 --> 00:45:10.360
which is really handy.

00:45:10.360 --> 00:45:14.633
So the one-way thing is
really important to remember.

00:45:14.633 --> 00:45:16.050
When you're
connecting everything,

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:18.570
if it's not working,
that's a hint.

00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:21.510
You'll notice that I drew
this LED with one leg longer

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:22.330
than the other.

00:45:22.330 --> 00:45:24.720
That's because
that's how they come,

00:45:24.720 --> 00:45:27.450
and that's a hint as to what
direction you should put them.

00:45:27.450 --> 00:45:29.100
So you can do that
by trial and error,

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:30.600
but once you figure
it out, then you

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:33.630
can write down the longer
leg, what direction that

00:45:33.630 --> 00:45:36.280
needs to go in.

00:45:36.280 --> 00:45:40.650
So we know that our battery
is a 9-volt battery.

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:41.580
Awesome.

00:45:41.580 --> 00:45:46.845
We know that our LED requires
20 milliamp hours and 3.6 volts.

00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:51.780
And we know we've
got this equation.

00:45:51.780 --> 00:45:54.310
And I told you that
you need a resistor.

00:45:54.310 --> 00:45:56.010
So we're probably
looking at what

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:57.630
we want the resistor to be.

00:45:57.630 --> 00:46:00.720
So what do we do from here?

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:02.430
Yeah.

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:05.335
AUDIENCE: So you know that
you have the remaining

00:46:05.335 --> 00:46:07.710
voltage that you need to drop
before the circuit finishes

00:46:07.710 --> 00:46:08.580
off.

00:46:08.580 --> 00:46:12.597
So we need to drop off that
much V in your resistor.

00:46:12.597 --> 00:46:15.180
And you know what the current
is in the resistor in milliamps,

00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:18.273
so is that it?

00:46:18.273 --> 00:46:19.440
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, exactly.

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:21.680
Are there any questions?

00:46:21.680 --> 00:46:23.860
So, OK, cool.

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:25.610
So I'll explain the
assignment, but that's

00:46:25.610 --> 00:46:27.980
going to be very useful.

00:46:27.980 --> 00:46:30.620
All right, so that would
be another reason why

00:46:30.620 --> 00:46:35.120
you need to use that information
is if you don't use a resistor,

00:46:35.120 --> 00:46:37.850
you're going to blow your
LED, and you only get one.

00:46:37.850 --> 00:46:41.180
So you don't want to blow it.

00:46:41.180 --> 00:46:44.450
Power is equal to the
voltage times the current.

00:46:44.450 --> 00:46:48.020
That's another useful one.

00:46:48.020 --> 00:46:50.120
Does this look like
any other equation

00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:53.450
you might have seen in a
mechanics-- more oriented

00:46:53.450 --> 00:46:56.570
mechanics, what
power is equal to?

00:46:56.570 --> 00:47:01.297
AUDIENCE: I squared R.

00:47:01.297 --> 00:47:02.380
AMY BANZAERT: I squared R?

00:47:02.380 --> 00:47:03.297
Is that what you said?

00:47:03.297 --> 00:47:06.450
AUDIENCE: No, [INAUDIBLE]
would be heat, right?

00:47:06.450 --> 00:47:08.290
Like, power [INAUDIBLE]
would be heat.

00:47:08.290 --> 00:47:10.630
So VI just reminded me of
the eat equation [INAUDIBLE]..

00:47:10.630 --> 00:47:13.350
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:47:13.350 --> 00:47:19.360
And it does indeed
equal I square R.

00:47:19.360 --> 00:47:24.940
But in terms of mechanical,
power and work are?

00:47:28.597 --> 00:47:30.773
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] over time.

00:47:30.773 --> 00:47:32.190
AMY BANZAERT: Is
it over the time?

00:47:35.090 --> 00:47:36.110
Yeah, exactly.

00:47:36.110 --> 00:47:38.570
So I had to remind
myself, so I just froze.

00:47:38.570 --> 00:47:45.590
But, yeah, so power is--

00:47:45.590 --> 00:47:47.420
what's another way of
thinking about power?

00:47:47.420 --> 00:47:49.190
AUDIENCE: Work over time.

00:47:49.190 --> 00:47:51.740
AMY BANZAERT: Work
over time, yeah.

00:47:51.740 --> 00:47:52.775
And what's work?

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:57.390
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:57.390 --> 00:47:58.390
AMY BANZAERT: Say again?

00:47:58.390 --> 00:48:01.680
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] joules.

00:48:01.680 --> 00:48:05.140
AMY BANZAERT: Yep,
and what's a joule?

00:48:05.140 --> 00:48:06.520
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:06.520 --> 00:48:07.520
AMY BANZAERT: Say again?

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:12.090
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:12.090 --> 00:48:13.710
AMY BANZAERT: A newton-meter.

00:48:13.710 --> 00:48:15.050
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:15.050 --> 00:48:16.050
AMY BANZAERT: Say again?

00:48:16.050 --> 00:48:16.620
AUDIENCE: Over time.

00:48:16.620 --> 00:48:17.790
AMY BANZAERT:
Exactly, over time.

00:48:17.790 --> 00:48:18.665
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:18.665 --> 00:48:20.466
AMY BANZAERT: So-- excuse me?

00:48:20.466 --> 00:48:22.280
AUDIENCE: Force velocity.

00:48:22.280 --> 00:48:25.310
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, exactly.

00:48:25.310 --> 00:48:28.730
So however you get
to it, power is

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:32.350
equal to force times velocity,
and so which it's annoying

00:48:32.350 --> 00:48:34.850
because we only have so many
letters in the English language

00:48:34.850 --> 00:48:35.660
to work with.

00:48:35.660 --> 00:48:38.030
But this velocity is
different from that voltage.

00:48:38.030 --> 00:48:41.450
But you can think of
the voltage as a force,

00:48:41.450 --> 00:48:44.080
like pressure, and
the I like a current,

00:48:44.080 --> 00:48:45.770
sort of like a velocity.

00:48:45.770 --> 00:48:48.440
And so that's how to
get back to relating

00:48:48.440 --> 00:48:53.090
the mechanical side of things to
the electronic side of things.

00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:55.548
And something they never told
me until I got to grad school

00:48:55.548 --> 00:48:57.882
is all these equations you
use in electrical engineering

00:48:57.882 --> 00:49:00.500
and all the equations you use
mechanical engineering are all

00:49:00.500 --> 00:49:02.990
based on the same formulas
when you go back far

00:49:02.990 --> 00:49:04.130
enough in the physics.

00:49:04.130 --> 00:49:06.770
And so we're all just
talking about the same stuff.

00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:09.320
We're just using
different assumptions

00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:10.650
and different variables.

00:49:10.650 --> 00:49:13.460
So if you're more comfortable
in the mechanical world,

00:49:13.460 --> 00:49:16.430
figure out what it is
in the electrical world

00:49:16.430 --> 00:49:20.210
and do the translation in
your mind or vice versa.

00:49:20.210 --> 00:49:21.080
It's a nice trick.

00:49:24.880 --> 00:49:27.200
If these are totally
unfamiliar concepts.

00:49:27.200 --> 00:49:29.080
These are two books
that I posted on Stellar

00:49:29.080 --> 00:49:31.330
that I would
recommend grabbing out

00:49:31.330 --> 00:49:33.940
of the library to be
a refresher, starting

00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:36.580
with the Scherz book.

00:49:36.580 --> 00:49:38.860
So a lot of this
class, we're going

00:49:38.860 --> 00:49:45.100
to do estimation because it's
a really handy tool for doing

00:49:45.100 --> 00:49:49.060
work in developing countries
and as engineers and everywhere.

00:49:49.060 --> 00:49:52.360
So we're going to start
with how to estimate

00:49:52.360 --> 00:49:55.210
the energy in that
9-volt battery

00:49:55.210 --> 00:49:57.590
that I'm about to give you
that we just talked about.

00:49:57.590 --> 00:50:03.130
So here's the 9-volt battery.

00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:06.280
And how do we estimate
the energy stored in it?

00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:15.050
AUDIENCE: That's how long
it would power a flashlight.

00:50:15.050 --> 00:50:21.110
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, how
long to power something

00:50:21.110 --> 00:50:24.620
like a flashlight.

00:50:24.620 --> 00:50:26.150
So let's go down that path.

00:50:29.820 --> 00:50:33.270
So what do we need to think
about in order to figure out--

00:50:33.270 --> 00:50:34.888
so the reason I
put that underscore

00:50:34.888 --> 00:50:37.430
there is just because you could
think about how long it takes

00:50:37.430 --> 00:50:38.690
to power something
else if you know

00:50:38.690 --> 00:50:40.010
that better than a flashlight.

00:50:40.010 --> 00:50:42.510
But, yeah, this is a really
good way to think about that.

00:50:42.510 --> 00:50:46.400
So what do we need to think
about in order to know that?

00:50:46.400 --> 00:50:48.170
So how long does a
9-volt battery power

00:50:48.170 --> 00:50:51.105
your flashlight for?

00:50:51.105 --> 00:50:53.105
AUDIENCE: It's-- you need
to know how much power

00:50:53.105 --> 00:50:56.770
the flashlight [INAUDIBLE].

00:50:56.770 --> 00:50:59.963
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah so how could
we get a rough number for that?

00:50:59.963 --> 00:51:01.588
AUDIENCE: It's less--
it should be less

00:51:01.588 --> 00:51:04.203
than a light bulb [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:04.203 --> 00:51:06.120
AMY BANZAERT: OK, but
it's still a light bulb.

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:07.710
AUDIENCE: The light
bulb [INAUDIBLE]..

00:51:07.710 --> 00:51:09.210
AMY BANZAERT: So a
small light bulb.

00:51:09.210 --> 00:51:15.870
So we are powering
a little light bulb.

00:51:15.870 --> 00:51:19.470
So what do you think that
light bulb might be rated at?

00:51:23.110 --> 00:51:23.610
Hm?

00:51:23.610 --> 00:51:24.880
AUDIENCE: 20 watts.

00:51:24.880 --> 00:51:27.310
AMY BANZAERT: 20 watts.

00:51:27.310 --> 00:51:30.124
Anyone else have a
different thought?

00:51:30.124 --> 00:51:31.282
AUDIENCE: 10.

00:51:31.282 --> 00:51:31.990
AMY BANZAERT: 10?

00:51:35.360 --> 00:51:38.180
All right, so generally
in estimation, you

00:51:38.180 --> 00:51:39.890
go with orders of
magnitude, like

00:51:39.890 --> 00:51:41.160
we're talking about before.

00:51:41.160 --> 00:51:44.060
So if you know specifically that
you're using a 20-watt light

00:51:44.060 --> 00:51:45.380
bulb, put that 2 in.

00:51:45.380 --> 00:51:49.100
But if you don't, just
go with 10 or 1 or 100.

00:51:49.100 --> 00:51:52.875
And then, hopefully,
the small digits

00:51:52.875 --> 00:51:54.000
will cancel each other out.

00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:56.030
But in estimations,
we're never trying--

00:51:56.030 --> 00:51:57.650
at this level,
we're never trying

00:51:57.650 --> 00:51:59.660
to get to an actual
exact number that you're

00:51:59.660 --> 00:52:01.760
going to use in a
specific equation

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:05.340
that you're going to use to
actually design a flashlight.

00:52:05.340 --> 00:52:07.340
You're just trying to get
an order of magnitude.

00:52:07.340 --> 00:52:10.160
Can I power a flashlight or
can I power this light bulb

00:52:10.160 --> 00:52:12.620
with a voltmeter
battery, or do I

00:52:12.620 --> 00:52:15.210
need a bigger battery,
type of thing.

00:52:15.210 --> 00:52:17.480
So let's go with 10 watts.

00:52:17.480 --> 00:52:21.420
So we know the flashlight
or the light bulb is 10.

00:52:21.420 --> 00:52:24.860
We think the light
bulb is 10 watts.

00:52:24.860 --> 00:52:25.415
What's next?

00:52:29.510 --> 00:52:30.080
Someone else.

00:52:30.080 --> 00:52:31.980
[LAUGHS] No, you're doing great.

00:52:31.980 --> 00:52:32.480
Yeah?

00:52:32.480 --> 00:52:34.450
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] the
amount of time it takes.

00:52:34.450 --> 00:52:35.658
AMY BANZAERT: OK, yeah, yeah.

00:52:35.658 --> 00:52:37.610
So let's multiply it by time.

00:52:37.610 --> 00:52:41.456
And then what will we have?

00:52:41.456 --> 00:52:44.020
AUDIENCE: Energy
[INAUDIBLE] multiplied.

00:52:44.020 --> 00:52:45.770
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
we'll have our energy.

00:52:45.770 --> 00:52:46.740
Awesome.

00:52:46.740 --> 00:52:49.610
So how long does
your flashlight last?

00:52:54.910 --> 00:52:56.910
AUDIENCE: 10 hours.

00:52:56.910 --> 00:52:58.690
AMY BANZAERT: 10 hours?

00:52:58.690 --> 00:53:01.170
So what happens if I do this?

00:53:01.170 --> 00:53:02.670
What's wrong?

00:53:02.670 --> 00:53:03.635
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:03.635 --> 00:53:05.760
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, my
units have been totally off.

00:53:05.760 --> 00:53:07.720
So 10 hours.

00:53:07.720 --> 00:53:09.000
So what units do I want?

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:09.990
AUDIENCE: Seconds.

00:53:09.990 --> 00:53:15.150
AMY BANZAERT:
Yeah, so 60 minutes

00:53:15.150 --> 00:53:21.540
per hour times 60
seconds per minute

00:53:21.540 --> 00:53:29.270
is 3,600 times 10 times 10.

00:53:29.270 --> 00:53:30.400
So what are my units now?

00:53:30.400 --> 00:53:31.390
AUDIENCE: Joules.

00:53:31.390 --> 00:53:33.210
AMY BANZAERT: Joules, yeah.

00:53:33.210 --> 00:53:34.110
Awesome.

00:53:34.110 --> 00:53:36.240
So does that sound good?

00:53:39.020 --> 00:53:42.200
So 36 kilojoules.

00:53:42.200 --> 00:53:45.350
When I did this, the
way I did it is I've

00:53:45.350 --> 00:53:48.920
never had a flashlight
that uses a 9-volt battery.

00:53:48.920 --> 00:53:52.730
I only have flashlights that
run on D cells or AA cells

00:53:52.730 --> 00:53:53.450
or whatever.

00:53:53.450 --> 00:53:55.070
So I didn't really
have a good sense

00:53:55.070 --> 00:53:58.860
of how long my light bulb
would last on 10 hours.

00:53:58.860 --> 00:54:02.090
And so I estimated that--

00:54:02.090 --> 00:54:04.430
well, first, I thought
about how a battery works.

00:54:04.430 --> 00:54:08.660
And how does a battery
work in really simple

00:54:08.660 --> 00:54:11.508
10-year-old terms?

00:54:11.508 --> 00:54:12.505
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:54:12.505 --> 00:54:14.630
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so
there's a chemical reaction,

00:54:14.630 --> 00:54:20.510
which requires chemicals,
which are volume based, right?

00:54:20.510 --> 00:54:24.470
Like, you can
change the layering

00:54:24.470 --> 00:54:27.080
of the different plates
that you need in a battery.

00:54:27.080 --> 00:54:28.760
But fundamentally,
you can only have

00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:30.420
so many chemicals in a battery.

00:54:30.420 --> 00:54:35.300
So I thought about it like two
AAs is about the same volume,

00:54:35.300 --> 00:54:36.410
and so it's equivalent.

00:54:36.410 --> 00:54:46.180
And then, therefore, I said, all
right, well, my flashlights--

00:54:46.180 --> 00:54:49.450
I forget how long I
thought they lasted.

00:54:49.450 --> 00:54:52.060
I thought my bulb
was actually 5 watts,

00:54:52.060 --> 00:54:56.500
so I cheated and put 5 in
there instead of a 10 or a 1.

00:54:56.500 --> 00:55:00.580
I thought it lasted
just an hour.

00:55:00.580 --> 00:55:07.750
So I got this many
joules, which I

00:55:07.750 --> 00:55:12.040
believe is actually closer to
how long a 9-volt will last,

00:55:12.040 --> 00:55:14.230
depending on how
good your 9-volt is.

00:55:14.230 --> 00:55:16.760
So you can see, but
you guys are still

00:55:16.760 --> 00:55:18.760
in the same order of
magnitude, so we're golden.

00:55:18.760 --> 00:55:21.040
It doesn't really matter,
but just to give you

00:55:21.040 --> 00:55:23.060
a slightly different approach.

00:55:23.060 --> 00:55:28.280
So now let's try to
talk about estimation.

00:55:28.280 --> 00:55:32.360
So why am I bugging you to
do this in an energy class?

00:55:32.360 --> 00:55:35.140
So one is that it's really
handy when you're in a meeting,

00:55:35.140 --> 00:55:36.390
say, with a community partner.

00:55:36.390 --> 00:55:38.170
And community
partner says, I want

00:55:38.170 --> 00:55:43.100
you to install me a solar
panel, and my roof is this big,

00:55:43.100 --> 00:55:46.960
and I want it to power all
the ovens for my kitchen.

00:55:46.960 --> 00:55:50.830
You might be able to very
quickly, rather than saying,

00:55:50.830 --> 00:55:54.147
I don't know, or
that sounds stupid,

00:55:54.147 --> 00:55:56.230
you might be able to very
quickly do the equations

00:55:56.230 --> 00:55:58.450
and say, actually,
you'd need a solar panel

00:55:58.450 --> 00:56:01.160
the size of a football
field to make that work.

00:56:01.160 --> 00:56:03.558
So let's think of
a different method.

00:56:03.558 --> 00:56:06.100
It's a really good way to gut
check your work or your others'

00:56:06.100 --> 00:56:07.773
work, so other people's work.

00:56:07.773 --> 00:56:09.940
So if you read in a newspaper
that someone's come up

00:56:09.940 --> 00:56:13.300
with a new magic energy source
that does this amazing stuff,

00:56:13.300 --> 00:56:15.950
you can really quickly check and
see, does this make any sense,

00:56:15.950 --> 00:56:17.890
or is the reporter not
doing due diligence?

00:56:17.890 --> 00:56:21.190
Or when you come up with
an answer on a homework set

00:56:21.190 --> 00:56:23.600
or design problem, find it out.

00:56:23.600 --> 00:56:25.360
And then also saving
time and money

00:56:25.360 --> 00:56:30.310
that you don't have to spend
the time to figure out that--

00:56:30.310 --> 00:56:33.010
spec that whole solar system
that that community partner

00:56:33.010 --> 00:56:34.960
wanted before you've
actually figured out,

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:39.310
is the solar panel going
to work at all for you?

00:56:39.310 --> 00:56:41.830
Estimation tends to
feel really hard,

00:56:41.830 --> 00:56:45.200
and that's really legitimate.

00:56:45.200 --> 00:56:48.880
So there's a lot of reasons
that that's the case.

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:51.340
One is that you're used to
problem sets, where there's

00:56:51.340 --> 00:56:54.245
generally a right answer, and
exams, where there's generally

00:56:54.245 --> 00:56:54.870
a right answer.

00:56:54.870 --> 00:56:56.680
Certainly, that was
true in high school

00:56:56.680 --> 00:56:59.350
and often is true even at MIT.

00:56:59.350 --> 00:57:01.720
And in estimation there's
kind of a right answer,

00:57:01.720 --> 00:57:03.010
but there's a lot of
different assumptions

00:57:03.010 --> 00:57:05.385
you can make that could give
you a lot of different right

00:57:05.385 --> 00:57:06.790
answers.

00:57:06.790 --> 00:57:09.250
You're also used
to defined realms,

00:57:09.250 --> 00:57:11.890
where you know you're
going to be given a test,

00:57:11.890 --> 00:57:14.290
and it's going to test
you on these 10 equations

00:57:14.290 --> 00:57:16.240
and how to use them
in these five realms,

00:57:16.240 --> 00:57:17.810
and that's all you need to know.

00:57:17.810 --> 00:57:19.750
And this is out of
that comfort zone

00:57:19.750 --> 00:57:22.060
and totally in a
different space.

00:57:22.060 --> 00:57:24.340
You typically have no
resources beyond your brain,

00:57:24.340 --> 00:57:26.470
so, yes, we have Google.

00:57:26.470 --> 00:57:27.970
You can always use that.

00:57:27.970 --> 00:57:29.470
But in this class,
we're often going

00:57:29.470 --> 00:57:30.940
to be doing it with no Google.

00:57:30.940 --> 00:57:32.900
And so all you
have is your brain.

00:57:32.900 --> 00:57:35.980
And so it's nice to be able to
lean on a textbook or Google

00:57:35.980 --> 00:57:37.510
or whatever.

00:57:37.510 --> 00:57:38.960
It's often in public.

00:57:38.960 --> 00:57:40.930
So there's little time,
there's little warning.

00:57:40.930 --> 00:57:42.070
That can be terrifying.

00:57:42.070 --> 00:57:43.180
You'll see me screw up.

00:57:43.180 --> 00:57:44.020
You may screw up.

00:57:44.020 --> 00:57:45.370
It's hard.

00:57:45.370 --> 00:57:49.760
But it's really worthwhile,
as I talked before.

00:57:49.760 --> 00:57:50.860
Again, it's open-ended.

00:57:50.860 --> 00:57:52.180
It's ill-defined.

00:57:52.180 --> 00:57:55.270
There's multiple acceptable
methods and answers.

00:57:55.270 --> 00:57:58.510
The significant
uncertainty-- is a light bulb

00:57:58.510 --> 00:58:03.070
actually 5 watts or 10
watts or 1 watt or bigger?

00:58:03.070 --> 00:58:05.140
It's hard to know exactly
if you can't actually

00:58:05.140 --> 00:58:09.760
look at the light bulb,
and that can be stressful.

00:58:09.760 --> 00:58:13.060
And then you have to
have the set of knowledge

00:58:13.060 --> 00:58:16.360
that you don't just know values
or quantities or relationships,

00:58:16.360 --> 00:58:18.890
but you know all of them
in relation to each other,

00:58:18.890 --> 00:58:21.350
and that is a lot of knowledge.

00:58:21.350 --> 00:58:23.080
So that's one of
the reasons that I'm

00:58:23.080 --> 00:58:26.680
trying to push you to have
these units at your fingertips

00:58:26.680 --> 00:58:28.630
as well as having
some quantities,

00:58:28.630 --> 00:58:30.920
like how much is an
apple, things like that,

00:58:30.920 --> 00:58:32.410
so that you're
going to be better

00:58:32.410 --> 00:58:34.285
at estimation because
part of it is practice,

00:58:34.285 --> 00:58:36.827
and practice is huge, and we're
going to be doing that a ton.

00:58:36.827 --> 00:58:39.010
And the only way to get
decent at it is to practice.

00:58:39.010 --> 00:58:42.250
But part of it is
having those quantities

00:58:42.250 --> 00:58:44.950
in your head already so that
some of that uncertainty

00:58:44.950 --> 00:58:47.180
disappears.

00:58:47.180 --> 00:58:49.360
So what are the actions
that you can take

00:58:49.360 --> 00:58:51.500
to do effective estimation?

00:58:51.500 --> 00:58:54.520
So identifying a problem
system-- so that generally

00:58:54.520 --> 00:58:56.840
involves drawing these pictures.

00:58:56.840 --> 00:58:58.758
So I drew my
battery, then I know

00:58:58.758 --> 00:59:01.300
it needs to power a light bulb,
and I know they're connected.

00:59:01.300 --> 00:59:03.820
And that is a good way
just to think about it.

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:08.780
Drawing your system always seems
to help in problem solving.

00:59:08.780 --> 00:59:10.940
Identifying a quantity
within a system--

00:59:10.940 --> 00:59:15.730
so in this case, we identified
that there's a battery,

00:59:15.730 --> 00:59:16.810
and it's 9 volts.

00:59:16.810 --> 00:59:21.820
And we identified a 10-watt
or 5-watt light bulb.

00:59:21.820 --> 00:59:23.600
Providing a value
for the quantity--

00:59:23.600 --> 00:59:25.817
so I kind of combined
those two, right?

00:59:25.817 --> 00:59:27.400
But you could think,
all right, I need

00:59:27.400 --> 00:59:28.360
to know the voltage for this.

00:59:28.360 --> 00:59:29.818
I need to know the
wattage for that

00:59:29.818 --> 00:59:31.207
and then think about what it is.

00:59:31.207 --> 00:59:33.040
And then also with time,
we really did that.

00:59:33.040 --> 00:59:34.957
We thought about time,
and then we figured out

00:59:34.957 --> 00:59:35.980
what it needed to be.

00:59:35.980 --> 00:59:37.790
Counting a set of things.

00:59:37.790 --> 00:59:41.590
So I'm trying to think
of a good example

00:59:41.590 --> 00:59:42.970
for counting a set of things.

00:59:48.480 --> 00:59:51.530
So, for example, if you're
estimating how many seats there

00:59:51.530 --> 00:59:55.760
are in 10 to 50, you could count
a row and then multiply, right?

00:59:55.760 --> 00:59:59.553
Or if you're estimating how
many light bulbs in a kilowatt,

00:59:59.553 --> 01:00:01.095
you count one, and
then you multiply.

01:00:04.580 --> 01:00:07.400
Comparing two systems
for a quantity-- so

01:00:07.400 --> 01:00:11.300
I compared my AAs to
a 9 volt because I'm

01:00:11.300 --> 01:00:14.570
more comfortable thinking
in AAs than I am in 9 volts.

01:00:14.570 --> 01:00:17.330
Every time you do that, you're
making another assumption.

01:00:17.330 --> 01:00:19.010
It's a little risky,
but it's better

01:00:19.010 --> 01:00:24.020
than doing nothing by far.

01:00:24.020 --> 01:00:28.850
Identifying a relationship
between quantities-- so

01:00:28.850 --> 01:00:29.570
we did that.

01:00:29.570 --> 01:00:32.900
Power times rate is energy.

01:00:32.900 --> 01:00:35.750
And then we have
changing a system scope.

01:00:35.750 --> 01:00:40.420
So that's what I did
getting from 9 volts to AAs

01:00:40.420 --> 01:00:44.890
and identifying
a similar system.

01:00:44.890 --> 01:00:46.750
Oh, I guess scope
could also be--

01:00:46.750 --> 01:00:49.660
or similar system could be
thinking about this chemically.

01:00:49.660 --> 01:00:51.710
Like, you could skip all
these energy equations.

01:00:51.710 --> 01:00:53.320
And if you are a
chemist and really

01:00:53.320 --> 01:00:55.070
comfortable with
chemistry, you could just

01:00:55.070 --> 01:00:57.580
figure out the chemical
reaction and do it that way.

01:00:57.580 --> 01:01:00.100
I'm not a chemist, so that's
not the way I'm going to pick,

01:01:00.100 --> 01:01:03.070
but it would be
another approach.

01:01:03.070 --> 01:01:05.560
And then, as I
mentioned, practice.

01:01:05.560 --> 01:01:11.830
OK, so now we're going to do
a harder estimation together,

01:01:11.830 --> 01:01:15.400
and then we're going to
take a break because I

01:01:15.400 --> 01:01:17.890
know three hours is an
incredibly long time

01:01:17.890 --> 01:01:23.060
to sit on uncomfortable chairs
in a poorly laid-out classroom.

01:01:23.060 --> 01:01:30.016
So how do you want to
approach this bicycle problem?

01:01:30.016 --> 01:01:31.990
AUDIENCE: Should
we put [INAUDIBLE]??

01:01:31.990 --> 01:01:32.440
AMY BANZAERT: I'm sorry?

01:01:32.440 --> 01:01:33.100
AUDIENCE: The caption.

01:01:33.100 --> 01:01:33.550
AMY BANZAERT: Oh, yeah, yeah.

01:01:33.550 --> 01:01:35.590
So this says, "Estimate
the drag force

01:01:35.590 --> 01:01:39.002
on a bicycle and rider
traveling at 20 miles per hour."

01:01:39.002 --> 01:01:40.210
And I gave you a little help.

01:01:40.210 --> 01:01:41.365
That's 9 meters per second.

01:01:44.938 --> 01:01:48.256
AUDIENCE: By drag, do you mean
the wind as well as the ground

01:01:48.256 --> 01:01:50.125
[INAUDIBLE] friction?

01:01:50.125 --> 01:01:52.000
AMY BANZAERT: So what
do you think is bigger?

01:01:52.000 --> 01:01:53.005
AUDIENCE: The ground.

01:01:53.005 --> 01:01:54.880
AMY BANZAERT: You think
the ground is bigger?

01:01:54.880 --> 01:01:56.880
AUDIENCE: He's traveling
at 9 meters per second.

01:01:56.880 --> 01:02:00.156
AMY BANZAERT: Does anyone
have a different approach?

01:02:00.156 --> 01:02:03.828
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:03.828 --> 01:02:06.120
AMY BANZAERT: So I think the
wind is going to dominate.

01:02:06.120 --> 01:02:10.790
And so you could include
the wheel friction.

01:02:10.790 --> 01:02:12.788
But in an estimation,
you want to just look

01:02:12.788 --> 01:02:13.830
at your biggest quantity.

01:02:13.830 --> 01:02:16.010
So I'm going to say we
can neglect that friction

01:02:16.010 --> 01:02:20.300
and just focus on the drag
associated with that body going

01:02:20.300 --> 01:02:21.020
against the wind.

01:02:21.020 --> 01:02:23.510
And this is a
question last year.

01:02:23.510 --> 01:02:25.280
This is just a
silly-looking bicycle.

01:02:25.280 --> 01:02:28.410
That's why I posted it,
because I find it amazing.

01:02:28.410 --> 01:02:31.760
But we don't need to worry about
whether the person is facing

01:02:31.760 --> 01:02:33.620
the wind this way or this way.

01:02:33.620 --> 01:02:37.040
We can assume the body--

01:02:37.040 --> 01:02:39.260
you can neglect that,
worrying about that.

01:02:39.260 --> 01:02:43.595
So what do we need
to think about?

01:02:43.595 --> 01:02:44.470
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:02:44.470 --> 01:02:46.892
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] surface
area of the [INAUDIBLE]..

01:02:46.892 --> 01:02:49.350
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so we can
think about the surface area.

01:02:49.350 --> 01:02:51.896
So how do you want
to model our person?

01:02:51.896 --> 01:02:53.710
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
a triangle.

01:02:53.710 --> 01:02:56.260
AMY BANZAERT: All
right, so a triangle.

01:02:56.260 --> 01:02:57.190
We can do a triangle.

01:02:57.190 --> 01:02:58.960
We could do a rectangle.

01:02:58.960 --> 01:03:01.420
The equation for
area of a rectangle

01:03:01.420 --> 01:03:03.380
is a little bit
easier than a triangle

01:03:03.380 --> 01:03:04.990
because you don't
have to divide by 2.

01:03:04.990 --> 01:03:07.600
So let's go with
rectangle because it's

01:03:07.600 --> 01:03:08.630
kind of meaningless.

01:03:08.630 --> 01:03:10.690
So what's the area
of an average person?

01:03:13.942 --> 01:03:15.775
AUDIENCE: Half a meter
squared [INAUDIBLE]..

01:03:15.775 --> 01:03:17.233
AMY BANZAERT: Half
a meter squared.

01:03:17.233 --> 01:03:19.050
OK, great.

01:03:19.050 --> 01:03:20.760
So we have our surface area.

01:03:23.460 --> 01:03:26.880
Does anyone know how surface
area relates to drag force?

01:03:31.080 --> 01:03:32.955
AUDIENCE: There's usually
a drag coefficient,

01:03:32.955 --> 01:03:35.710
and you just want to
[INAUDIBLE] area [INAUDIBLE]..

01:03:35.710 --> 01:03:37.980
AMY BANZAERT: So
the drag force is

01:03:37.980 --> 01:03:43.098
equal to a drag
coefficient times the area.

01:03:43.098 --> 01:03:44.526
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:47.150 --> 01:03:48.150
AMY BANZAERT: Say again?

01:03:48.150 --> 01:03:50.002
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
times velocity.

01:03:50.002 --> 01:03:51.210
AMY BANZAERT: Times velocity.

01:04:00.930 --> 01:04:04.720
Does anyone know that
drag coefficient?

01:04:04.720 --> 01:04:06.960
So this might not be
the best approach.

01:04:06.960 --> 01:04:10.020
All right, so this
was step one--

01:04:10.020 --> 01:04:11.730
didn't work.

01:04:11.730 --> 01:04:14.140
Or it's one that
we could go after,

01:04:14.140 --> 01:04:15.978
but you're trying to
answer this question

01:04:15.978 --> 01:04:18.270
right now in the classroom
without looking up that drag

01:04:18.270 --> 01:04:18.882
coefficient.

01:04:18.882 --> 01:04:20.340
And we don't have
any people who've

01:04:20.340 --> 01:04:22.260
been spending too much
time on fluid mechanics,

01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:24.300
so we're going to have to go
a different approach, which

01:04:24.300 --> 01:04:24.847
is great.

01:04:24.847 --> 01:04:26.430
So what would be a
different approach?

01:04:26.430 --> 01:04:28.382
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] density.

01:04:28.382 --> 01:04:29.358
The density variable.

01:04:29.358 --> 01:04:32.530
So kilograms per meter cubed.

01:04:32.530 --> 01:04:34.970
So if you know how many--

01:04:34.970 --> 01:04:39.150
the volume [INAUDIBLE]
flow that's [INAUDIBLE]..

01:04:39.150 --> 01:04:42.510
AMY BANZAERT: So we have
kilograms per meter cubed.

01:04:42.510 --> 01:04:45.720
So you're saying to multiply it
by area to get kilograms from--

01:04:45.720 --> 01:04:46.950
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:04:46.950 --> 01:04:47.950
AMY BANZAERT: I'm sorry?

01:04:47.950 --> 01:04:50.305
AUDIENCE: Multiply by
the velocity [INAUDIBLE]

01:04:50.305 --> 01:04:51.275
the volume metric.

01:04:51.275 --> 01:04:52.110
AMY BANZAERT: Mhm.

01:04:52.110 --> 01:04:56.820
So I think we're
actually here, still.

01:04:56.820 --> 01:05:00.150
So now we're getting at
an equation that actually

01:05:00.150 --> 01:05:04.080
has the right units, so
that's great because we've

01:05:04.080 --> 01:05:07.200
added in the density.

01:05:07.200 --> 01:05:10.320
And we could say that
this is just a constant,

01:05:10.320 --> 01:05:12.730
and we're doing an estimation,
and so we can neglect it.

01:05:12.730 --> 01:05:14.180
So absolutely, this
would be one way.

01:05:14.180 --> 01:05:15.055
So let's work it out.

01:05:15.055 --> 01:05:17.852
So does anyone know
the density of air?

01:05:17.852 --> 01:05:19.540
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:05:19.540 --> 01:05:22.450
AMY BANZAERT: It's
basically 1, yep.

01:05:22.450 --> 01:05:28.450
And then we have our area
is 1/2, and velocity is 9.

01:05:28.450 --> 01:05:30.115
So that's basically 5.

01:05:32.650 --> 01:05:35.140
5 whats?

01:05:35.140 --> 01:05:36.160
Forces in?

01:05:36.160 --> 01:05:36.940
AUDIENCE: Newtons.

01:05:36.940 --> 01:05:38.950
AMY BANZAERT:
Yeah, so 5 newtons.

01:05:38.950 --> 01:05:42.142
So how can you gut
check if 5 newtons

01:05:42.142 --> 01:05:43.225
sounds like a good number?

01:05:49.555 --> 01:05:50.890
OK, so what is a newton?

01:05:50.890 --> 01:05:52.120
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:05:52.120 --> 01:05:53.537
AMY BANZAERT: An
apple, all right.

01:05:53.537 --> 01:05:55.600
So we're talking about
the weight of five apples.

01:05:55.600 --> 01:05:59.530
When you ride on a bike
at 20 miles an hour,

01:05:59.530 --> 01:06:01.433
is that fast or slow on a bike?

01:06:01.433 --> 01:06:02.266
AUDIENCE: It's fast.

01:06:02.266 --> 01:06:02.678
AUDIENCE: Fast.

01:06:02.678 --> 01:06:03.090
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:03.090 --> 01:06:04.715
AMY BANZAERT: It's
pretty decent, yeah.

01:06:04.715 --> 01:06:07.570
So how many people have
ridden a bike before?

01:06:07.570 --> 01:06:08.440
All right, awesome.

01:06:08.440 --> 01:06:11.680
So this is-- we're getting
into familiar territory, great.

01:06:11.680 --> 01:06:14.770
So when you're riding
on a bike, does it

01:06:14.770 --> 01:06:18.430
feel like it could be
5 newtons on your body?

01:06:18.430 --> 01:06:20.170
Five apples?

01:06:20.170 --> 01:06:23.860
OK, so if I said, could it
be 500 apples, would you--

01:06:23.860 --> 01:06:24.790
that's ridiculous.

01:06:24.790 --> 01:06:28.848
If I said, like, zero apples,
you'd probably say, no.

01:06:28.848 --> 01:06:30.640
You're feeling something
against your body.

01:06:30.640 --> 01:06:32.230
So maybe we're in
the right realm.

01:06:32.230 --> 01:06:33.830
Cool.

01:06:33.830 --> 01:06:34.330
Great.

01:06:34.330 --> 01:06:35.410
So good job.

01:06:35.410 --> 01:06:37.330
A lot of people give
up on this approach

01:06:37.330 --> 01:06:43.810
because you can't remember
that constant or whatever.

01:06:43.810 --> 01:06:45.820
Or you're missing
a term in this one

01:06:45.820 --> 01:06:47.650
because we need
that row in there.

01:06:47.650 --> 01:06:48.790
But this one worked.

01:06:48.790 --> 01:06:53.230
The way I do it, the
way I approached it,

01:06:53.230 --> 01:06:59.440
is I thought about the power
associated with bike riding.

01:06:59.440 --> 01:07:01.930
So when I bike at
the Z Center, it

01:07:01.930 --> 01:07:07.660
tells me actually how
much work I'm doing,

01:07:07.660 --> 01:07:09.220
how much power I'm generating.

01:07:09.220 --> 01:07:13.150
And so I'm kind of
a terrible biker.

01:07:13.150 --> 01:07:15.250
You can tell I'm a
little out of shape.

01:07:15.250 --> 01:07:19.210
But I know that I
can bike somewhere

01:07:19.210 --> 01:07:22.490
on the order of 200 watts.

01:07:22.490 --> 01:07:25.900
And so that means
I'm all set, right?

01:07:25.900 --> 01:07:28.960
Because now I have
that and now I just

01:07:28.960 --> 01:07:30.880
need my 9 meters per second.

01:07:30.880 --> 01:07:36.293
And what am I doing wrong?

01:07:36.293 --> 01:07:37.460
That's what I'm doing wrong.

01:07:37.460 --> 01:07:38.168
I need to divide.

01:07:38.168 --> 01:07:44.170
[LAUGHS] And so then I
get about 20 newtons.

01:07:47.300 --> 01:07:48.020
Is that right?

01:07:48.020 --> 01:07:49.760
Did I do that math right?

01:07:49.760 --> 01:07:51.065
Sorry, board fatigue.

01:07:51.065 --> 01:07:52.820
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:07:52.820 --> 01:07:53.658
AMY BANZAERT: What?

01:07:53.658 --> 01:07:55.180
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
that's about right.

01:07:55.180 --> 01:07:56.180
AMY BANZAERT: Thank you.

01:07:56.180 --> 01:07:58.805
Yeah, I know I'm not
actually dividing 9.

01:07:58.805 --> 01:08:00.680
I'm dividing 10 because
when it's estimation,

01:08:00.680 --> 01:08:02.070
it doesn't matter.

01:08:02.070 --> 01:08:05.000
So we're kind of in the
same order of magnitude.

01:08:05.000 --> 01:08:07.590
We're a little different.

01:08:07.590 --> 01:08:09.775
But this is a nice
approach because it's just

01:08:09.775 --> 01:08:11.150
two quantities
and all you really

01:08:11.150 --> 01:08:12.320
have to think about is one.

01:08:12.320 --> 01:08:19.700
And this 200 watts is
actually ambitious for biking

01:08:19.700 --> 01:08:21.120
for a long period.

01:08:21.120 --> 01:08:23.089
That's a lot of power
you're outputting.

01:08:23.089 --> 01:08:26.092
AUDIENCE: How did you
know it was 200 watts?

01:08:26.092 --> 01:08:27.050
AMY BANZAERT: Because--

01:08:27.050 --> 01:08:27.560
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

01:08:27.560 --> 01:08:29.700
AMY BANZAERT: --you actually
see it on the screen.

01:08:29.700 --> 01:08:31.819
And some people who
are really into biking,

01:08:31.819 --> 01:08:35.330
like what Lance
Armstrong can do and what

01:08:35.330 --> 01:08:39.685
a normal person can do, yeah.

01:08:39.685 --> 01:08:41.060
So this is just
a nice way to see

01:08:41.060 --> 01:08:43.370
there's two different
approaches to this problem.

01:08:43.370 --> 01:08:46.100
And I'm sure there's others
that you could go after too.

01:08:46.100 --> 01:08:48.770
So there's always another way.

01:08:48.770 --> 01:08:52.189
Estimate how high a
D cell can lift you,

01:08:52.189 --> 01:08:56.000
and if you have a background
that's not engineering based,

01:08:56.000 --> 01:08:58.580
if you can find
someone who is, that

01:08:58.580 --> 01:09:02.180
might be good to have the mix
rather than two nonengineers

01:09:02.180 --> 01:09:04.770
working together on this,
though you guys may do fine too.

01:09:04.770 --> 01:09:06.380
So it's worth a shot.

01:09:06.380 --> 01:09:11.794
So, yeah, so take 10 minutes
to work on that problem.

01:09:15.031 --> 01:09:17.047
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

01:09:21.430 --> 01:09:24.850
OK, does every
team have a guess?

01:09:24.850 --> 01:09:26.372
Does any team not have a guess?

01:09:26.372 --> 01:09:28.330
Or can you raise your
hand if you have a guess?

01:09:28.330 --> 01:09:31.540
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:09:31.540 --> 01:09:32.560
AMY BANZAERT: OK, great.

01:09:32.560 --> 01:09:38.950
So did anyone have a
hundredth of a meter?

01:09:38.950 --> 01:09:40.420
Tenth?

01:09:40.420 --> 01:09:42.399
A meter?

01:09:42.399 --> 01:09:43.059
10 meters?

01:09:45.830 --> 01:09:49.079
So maybe a 16.

01:09:49.079 --> 01:09:51.270
100 meters?

01:09:51.270 --> 01:09:52.590
4.

01:09:52.590 --> 01:09:53.880
1,000?

01:09:53.880 --> 01:09:55.380
10,000?

01:09:55.380 --> 01:09:59.310
OK, so we're pretty constrained.

01:09:59.310 --> 01:10:02.437
How do these numbers strike
you, as reasonable, ridiculous?

01:10:02.437 --> 01:10:03.270
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:10:03.270 --> 01:10:04.145
AUDIENCE: Ridiculous.

01:10:04.145 --> 01:10:06.270
AMY BANZAERT: OK,
so, but there's

01:10:06.270 --> 01:10:08.730
an aspect to this that's
ridiculous, period.

01:10:08.730 --> 01:10:11.010
The question is a
little funny, right?

01:10:11.010 --> 01:10:12.280
We can talk about that.

01:10:12.280 --> 01:10:24.300
So can someone go through
how they got 10, order of 10?

01:10:27.210 --> 01:10:28.733
Yeah.

01:10:28.733 --> 01:10:30.150
AUDIENCE: So
basically what we did

01:10:30.150 --> 01:10:32.970
was we did the same thing
that you did earlier.

01:10:32.970 --> 01:10:34.890
We thought about
how long a D cell

01:10:34.890 --> 01:10:37.060
could power a 10-watt bulb.

01:10:37.060 --> 01:10:37.560
And--

01:10:37.560 --> 01:10:39.643
AMY BANZAERT: OK, so you
have your D cell system.

01:10:39.643 --> 01:10:40.310
AUDIENCE: Right.

01:10:40.310 --> 01:10:42.730
AMY BANZAERT: How
long, or let's do time

01:10:42.730 --> 01:10:44.483
to last for a 10-watt bulb.

01:10:44.483 --> 01:10:45.150
AUDIENCE: Right.

01:10:45.150 --> 01:10:45.858
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

01:10:45.858 --> 01:10:51.420
AUDIENCE: And we came up with
a number that, when we solved,

01:10:51.420 --> 01:10:53.865
we got 15,000 joules
as the energy.

01:10:59.820 --> 01:11:03.630
And then, basically,
we equated it

01:11:03.630 --> 01:11:05.940
with the potential
energy of a person

01:11:05.940 --> 01:11:09.678
weighing roughly 50
kilograms at a height x.

01:11:09.678 --> 01:11:10.470
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:11:10.470 --> 01:11:11.762
AUDIENCE: So that would be mgh.

01:11:11.762 --> 01:11:15.070
So that'd be 50 into 10 into x.

01:11:15.070 --> 01:11:18.210
And that would be
equated to 15,000.

01:11:18.210 --> 01:11:24.180
And then you solve for x,
and x turns out to be 30.

01:11:24.180 --> 01:11:27.440
AMY BANZAERT: OK, did anyone
take a different approach?

01:11:27.440 --> 01:11:30.362
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:11:30.362 --> 01:11:32.820
AMY BANZAERT: I kind of led
you guys down to this approach,

01:11:32.820 --> 01:11:35.580
given the previous
exercises, so I'm not shocked

01:11:35.580 --> 01:11:36.540
that no one did.

01:11:36.540 --> 01:11:38.397
For the people who
got closer to 100,

01:11:38.397 --> 01:11:40.980
what was the assumption that was
different that led you to 100

01:11:40.980 --> 01:11:45.080
rather than order
of magnitude 10?

01:11:45.080 --> 01:11:45.580
Yeah.

01:11:45.580 --> 01:11:48.820
AUDIENCE: Our battery, we put in
that it had 180,000 joules, not

01:11:48.820 --> 01:11:49.530
[INAUDIBLE].

01:11:49.530 --> 01:11:50.820
AMY BANZAERT: 180,000 joules?

01:11:50.820 --> 01:11:51.617
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:11:51.617 --> 01:11:53.700
AMY BANZAERT: And where
did that number come from?

01:11:56.318 --> 01:11:57.610
AUDIENCE: 10 watts for 5 hours.

01:11:57.610 --> 01:11:58.380
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:11:58.380 --> 01:12:01.950
AMY BANZAERT: So you guessed
10 watts times 5 hours

01:12:01.950 --> 01:12:02.910
got you there.

01:12:02.910 --> 01:12:06.000
So it was really
just because I think

01:12:06.000 --> 01:12:07.710
you guys had 10 watts
too, so it's just,

01:12:07.710 --> 01:12:10.380
how long does it last?

01:12:10.380 --> 01:12:14.490
15,000 joules is about
what the 9 volt lasted for.

01:12:14.490 --> 01:12:19.173
And how does the volume of a
D cell compare to a 9 volt?

01:12:19.173 --> 01:12:21.140
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:12:21.140 --> 01:12:24.590
AMY BANZAERT: So I would say
a D cell is actually maybe

01:12:24.590 --> 01:12:25.820
50% bigger.

01:12:25.820 --> 01:12:31.920
So I think 180,000
might be impressive,

01:12:31.920 --> 01:12:36.530
but 15,000 is a little
low, but we're all pretty

01:12:36.530 --> 01:12:38.240
close to the same ballpark.

01:12:41.990 --> 01:12:48.110
And I actually think that
we're closer to 100 than 10,

01:12:48.110 --> 01:12:50.270
depending on the
assumptions you make.

01:12:50.270 --> 01:12:53.700
But everyone did great
with these results.

01:12:53.700 --> 01:12:57.830
So why isn't there an
awesome personless lifter

01:12:57.830 --> 01:12:59.723
powered by a D cell
out there so we can all

01:12:59.723 --> 01:13:01.640
get to the top of our
roofs to shovel them off

01:13:01.640 --> 01:13:03.388
or whatever you need to do?

01:13:03.388 --> 01:13:05.180
You probably not
shoveling your dorm rooms,

01:13:05.180 --> 01:13:07.597
but I sure need to shovel my
roof if I could get up there.

01:13:07.597 --> 01:13:08.635
[LAUGHS]

01:13:08.635 --> 01:13:09.510
AUDIENCE: Efficiency.

01:13:09.510 --> 01:13:11.042
AMY BANZAERT: Efficiency, yup.

01:13:11.042 --> 01:13:12.750
AUDIENCE: It's also
instantaneous, right?

01:13:12.750 --> 01:13:15.950
You're assuming that
the battery [INAUDIBLE]..

01:13:15.950 --> 01:13:17.450
AMY BANZAERT: Well,
not necessarily.

01:13:17.450 --> 01:13:17.720
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:17.720 --> 01:13:19.850
AMY BANZAERT: I didn't
say how much time it took.

01:13:19.850 --> 01:13:22.820
AUDIENCE: That's true.

01:13:22.820 --> 01:13:27.320
AMY BANZAERT: So yeah, so there
are some efficiency issues.

01:13:27.320 --> 01:13:29.360
But this exercise
would actually run

01:13:29.360 --> 01:13:32.390
in a classical 2.009, which
is the senior mechanical

01:13:32.390 --> 01:13:33.770
engineering design class.

01:13:33.770 --> 01:13:37.580
And a guy called
Nate Ball came up--

01:13:37.580 --> 01:13:40.580
oh, I'm clicking on
the wrong computer--

01:13:40.580 --> 01:13:44.810
with what he calls the
Atlas Powered Rope Ascender

01:13:44.810 --> 01:13:47.817
that basically does this.

01:13:47.817 --> 01:13:49.650
Of course, there are
some efficiency losses,

01:13:49.650 --> 01:13:50.900
so it's not actually a D cell.

01:13:50.900 --> 01:13:52.500
It's a much better battery.

01:13:52.500 --> 01:13:55.350
But it's in kind of
the same ballpark.

01:13:55.350 --> 01:13:57.980
And so it's kind of amazing
because you wouldn't think

01:13:57.980 --> 01:14:01.190
that a D cell would have a
chance of lifting you anything,

01:14:01.190 --> 01:14:01.760
right?

01:14:01.760 --> 01:14:04.778
But the embodied energy in a D
cell is actually pretty good.

01:14:04.778 --> 01:14:06.320
And at the same
time, I'm telling you

01:14:06.320 --> 01:14:07.695
that I also showed
you that graph

01:14:07.695 --> 01:14:10.400
where I told you a battery is
lousy compared to gasoline.

01:14:10.400 --> 01:14:13.742
So it's good in some
respects, but it's also lousy

01:14:13.742 --> 01:14:15.950
if you actually wanted to
save power in an automobile

01:14:15.950 --> 01:14:20.150
with a D cell versus a person
lifting up a little bit.

01:14:20.150 --> 01:14:25.940
An automobile requires a
whole lot more energy to work.

01:14:25.940 --> 01:14:30.470
OK, so finally, after
all this other stuff,

01:14:30.470 --> 01:14:32.282
we can talk about the class now.

01:14:32.282 --> 01:14:36.500
Now you have a sense of what
the class is going to be like.

01:14:36.500 --> 01:14:38.440
So I'm Amy Banzaert.

01:14:38.440 --> 01:14:39.440
I have a three-year-old.

01:14:39.440 --> 01:14:40.340
His name is Eli.

01:14:40.340 --> 01:14:42.050
I do work in
developing countries,

01:14:42.050 --> 01:14:45.260
mostly on charcoal, which
you'll hear about later.

01:14:45.260 --> 01:14:46.505
Our TA is Amit Gandhi.

01:14:49.430 --> 01:14:51.020
Where are you there?

01:14:51.020 --> 01:14:52.270
AMIT GANDHI: I went to Brazil.

01:14:52.270 --> 01:14:53.570
AMY BANZAERT: Where?

01:14:53.570 --> 01:14:55.490
AMIT GANDHI: I went
to Brazil [INAUDIBLE]..

01:14:58.590 --> 01:15:00.640
AMY BANZAERT: I don't
know how I share this.

01:15:00.640 --> 01:15:01.390
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

01:15:01.390 --> 01:15:03.050
So I went to Brazil.

01:15:03.050 --> 01:15:05.810
And I've [INAUDIBLE] time
working in Ghana, Guatemala,

01:15:05.810 --> 01:15:06.590
and Brazil before.

01:15:10.170 --> 01:15:13.020
AMY BANZAERT: We have a bunch
of other staff who are helping.

01:15:13.020 --> 01:15:14.910
Dennis Nagle works downstairs.

01:15:14.910 --> 01:15:17.490
He's the shop manager for D-Lab.

01:15:17.490 --> 01:15:19.050
Steve Banzaert,
who you may notice

01:15:19.050 --> 01:15:21.750
the last name is
disturbingly similar,

01:15:21.750 --> 01:15:24.750
is, in fact, my husband, he's
also really good at EE stuff.

01:15:24.750 --> 01:15:27.360
So he helped teams last year,
and he'll do that again.

01:15:27.360 --> 01:15:31.530
Ken McEnaney is a mechanical
engineering grad student.

01:15:31.530 --> 01:15:34.080
Sarah Reed just finished
her master's here

01:15:34.080 --> 01:15:37.110
in Mech E, is really
good in design work.

01:15:37.110 --> 01:15:39.378
And then Amelia Servi,
also a mechanical engineer.

01:15:39.378 --> 01:15:40.920
Because this is a
design class, we're

01:15:40.920 --> 01:15:43.500
kind of slanted toward
mechanical engineering support.

01:15:43.500 --> 01:15:45.630
And then we'll probably
add a few other mentors

01:15:45.630 --> 01:15:46.470
along the way.

01:15:49.000 --> 01:15:51.390
So our syllabus--
so if you guys want

01:15:51.390 --> 01:15:56.333
to break out your
syllabus, you now

01:15:56.333 --> 01:15:57.750
know who's going
to help everyone.

01:15:57.750 --> 01:16:00.690
You can read the aims
and the objectives,

01:16:00.690 --> 01:16:03.030
which I have worked
pretty hard to make sure

01:16:03.030 --> 01:16:05.040
that everything
we're doing aligns

01:16:05.040 --> 01:16:06.870
with those aims and objectives.

01:16:06.870 --> 01:16:08.370
So that should give
you a good sense

01:16:08.370 --> 01:16:10.943
of what you can hope to
come out of the class with.

01:16:10.943 --> 01:16:12.360
And if those don't
align with what

01:16:12.360 --> 01:16:14.220
your hopes are for
the class, maybe this

01:16:14.220 --> 01:16:15.470
isn't the right class for you.

01:16:15.470 --> 01:16:18.630
And if they do, awesome,
I'm glad to have you.

01:16:18.630 --> 01:16:23.220
So this is a 12-unit
class, which means,

01:16:23.220 --> 01:16:25.740
hypothetically, you're spending
six hours a week in class

01:16:25.740 --> 01:16:27.240
and six hours a
week out of class,

01:16:27.240 --> 01:16:30.450
working on all the activities
associated with the class.

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:32.880
And last year was
a 9-unit class,

01:16:32.880 --> 01:16:35.250
and students said-- it
was very much 9 units.

01:16:35.250 --> 01:16:37.200
And they said there was
not nearly enough time

01:16:37.200 --> 01:16:39.742
to do everything they wanted to
do in the class, which is why

01:16:39.742 --> 01:16:40.909
we bumped it up to 12 units.

01:16:40.909 --> 01:16:43.242
So I think it is reasonable
to expect you'll be spending

01:16:43.242 --> 01:16:44.550
six hours a week out of class.

01:16:44.550 --> 01:16:47.920
And this is a class where
attendance is expected.

01:16:47.920 --> 01:16:50.520
So I know there's a
lot of classes at MIT

01:16:50.520 --> 01:16:53.040
where you can skip the lectures
and the labs, or at least

01:16:53.040 --> 01:16:54.330
the lectures, and do OK.

01:16:54.330 --> 01:16:56.953
In this class, like those
estimation exercises,

01:16:56.953 --> 01:16:59.370
you can't really capture what
we did in the lecture notes.

01:16:59.370 --> 01:17:01.953
And so you'll miss the learning
entirely if you don't show up.

01:17:01.953 --> 01:17:08.370
So there is a strong
punishment in terms of grades

01:17:08.370 --> 01:17:11.190
if you don't show up to class
without a really good reason

01:17:11.190 --> 01:17:15.960
why not in terms of grades
being reduced for those reasons.

01:17:15.960 --> 01:17:19.320
There are two quizzes that
happen in the beginning

01:17:19.320 --> 01:17:21.960
half of the term just
on the material covered

01:17:21.960 --> 01:17:25.950
in the class, the labs,
and the assignments,

01:17:25.950 --> 01:17:28.980
as well as assignments,
PSETs, lab stuff.

01:17:28.980 --> 01:17:31.860
Everything should be
turned on via Stellar

01:17:31.860 --> 01:17:33.990
unless it's something
that can't physically

01:17:33.990 --> 01:17:36.210
be turned in electronically.

01:17:36.210 --> 01:17:38.580
Last year, I had issues
with stuff getting lost,

01:17:38.580 --> 01:17:40.960
and so this is my hope
that nothing will get lost.

01:17:40.960 --> 01:17:43.110
So just make sure it
makes it on Stellar,

01:17:43.110 --> 01:17:47.490
and hopefully Stellar
won't disappoint.

01:17:47.490 --> 01:17:49.620
Are there any grad students?

01:17:49.620 --> 01:17:53.220
OK, so if you want
G-level credit as opposed

01:17:53.220 --> 01:17:55.380
to undergrad credit,
you need to sign up

01:17:55.380 --> 01:17:57.603
for a different subject number.

01:17:57.603 --> 01:17:59.520
So just you can talk to
me afterwards and just

01:17:59.520 --> 01:18:01.770
send me an email, telling
me that you're signing up

01:18:01.770 --> 01:18:04.800
for the grad class because,
if I don't know that,

01:18:04.800 --> 01:18:08.820
I will not be able to tell
the person who actually writes

01:18:08.820 --> 01:18:11.010
in your grade the
right grade, and then

01:18:11.010 --> 01:18:13.255
you'll get an F without
me even knowing that,

01:18:13.255 --> 01:18:14.130
and that would stink.

01:18:14.130 --> 01:18:16.950
So, yeah.

01:18:16.950 --> 01:18:18.450
Most of the readings
are going to be

01:18:18.450 --> 01:18:21.100
handed out online, basically.

01:18:21.100 --> 01:18:23.850
So while there is a
required text, which

01:18:23.850 --> 01:18:26.880
I think is a great text, of
Paul Polak's Out of Poverty,

01:18:26.880 --> 01:18:29.310
it's totally something that
you can borrow from a friend

01:18:29.310 --> 01:18:30.900
or grab from the
library because it's not

01:18:30.900 --> 01:18:32.442
like we're referring
to it every week

01:18:32.442 --> 01:18:35.310
and you need to have it heavily
highlighted and in your grasp

01:18:35.310 --> 01:18:39.630
at all times like you would
a math textbook or something.

01:18:39.630 --> 01:18:41.250
But Paul is a good
guy, so if you

01:18:41.250 --> 01:18:44.280
can afford the book,
which is, like, $14,

01:18:44.280 --> 01:18:46.110
it's worth supporting him.

01:18:46.110 --> 01:18:50.040
So the structure
of the class is--

01:18:50.040 --> 01:18:55.320
the first half of the class
is lecture and lab heavy.

01:18:55.320 --> 01:18:58.740
It's sort of like a normal
class, where, like today, I'll

01:18:58.740 --> 01:19:00.930
be doing talking, probably
not as much talking

01:19:00.930 --> 01:19:03.070
as I've done today ever again.

01:19:03.070 --> 01:19:06.630
We try to do a lot of hands-on
exercises in class too.

01:19:06.630 --> 01:19:08.490
But there will be a
decent bit of talking.

01:19:08.490 --> 01:19:12.060
And then the labs, which
are very hands on and run

01:19:12.060 --> 01:19:17.320
by Amit, where we
build a biodigester,

01:19:17.320 --> 01:19:21.270
we build a solar panel
out of solar cells,

01:19:21.270 --> 01:19:24.660
we build a little, tiny wind
turbine, you make charcoal,

01:19:24.660 --> 01:19:27.330
you do all stuff that's very
hands on, relevant to what

01:19:27.330 --> 01:19:30.300
we might be doing in Nicaragua.

01:19:30.300 --> 01:19:33.210
And then subsequently
over spring break,

01:19:33.210 --> 01:19:36.670
most people travel to
Nicaragua with the class,

01:19:36.670 --> 01:19:38.640
which is a really
fabulous experience.

01:19:38.640 --> 01:19:39.630
That's extracurricular.

01:19:39.630 --> 01:19:42.120
It's not a requirement of
the class to go to Nicaragua,

01:19:42.120 --> 01:19:45.990
but it does add a lot
to your experience.

01:19:45.990 --> 01:19:47.833
Actually working
with real people

01:19:47.833 --> 01:19:49.500
rather than them just
being hypothetical

01:19:49.500 --> 01:19:52.830
is a pretty valuable addition.

01:19:52.830 --> 01:19:55.260
And then after the
trip, you come back,

01:19:55.260 --> 01:19:57.270
and it's very much
a design class,

01:19:57.270 --> 01:19:59.820
where you're working on a
project identified in Nicaragua

01:19:59.820 --> 01:20:01.830
or really identified
beforehand that you

01:20:01.830 --> 01:20:03.210
learn more about in Nicaragua.

01:20:03.210 --> 01:20:06.540
And then you work on it for
the last half of the class.

01:20:06.540 --> 01:20:09.190
The lab and lectures
basically disappear,

01:20:09.190 --> 01:20:11.860
and it's all just work time
with a little bit of lecture

01:20:11.860 --> 01:20:14.600
content to help you
with the design process.

01:20:14.600 --> 01:20:16.340
So that part is
pretty intensive.

01:20:16.340 --> 01:20:18.940
And then you do
final presentations.

01:20:18.940 --> 01:20:21.920
We hand off the projects to
Nicaragua one way or another,

01:20:21.920 --> 01:20:23.330
and the class is over.

01:20:23.330 --> 01:20:28.240
So that's how it's split up.

01:20:28.240 --> 01:20:29.540
What else?

01:20:29.540 --> 01:20:32.350
One thing to know is, for people
who are going to Nicaragua,

01:20:32.350 --> 01:20:34.960
it's really helpful to
speak a bit of Spanish.

01:20:34.960 --> 01:20:38.380
And so one of the more
random homework assignments

01:20:38.380 --> 01:20:41.230
is to at least listen
to a couple of podcasts

01:20:41.230 --> 01:20:45.098
per week of a Spanish podcast,
like Spanish learning podcast,

01:20:45.098 --> 01:20:47.140
so that you can at least
say "hi" and "thank you"

01:20:47.140 --> 01:20:48.040
and things like that.

01:20:48.040 --> 01:20:52.150
If you happen to speak
Spanish already, bonus.

01:20:52.150 --> 01:20:54.550
And if you're fluent,
no need to practice.

01:20:54.550 --> 01:20:58.180
But if you're not, it's nice
to get a little practice.

01:20:58.180 --> 01:21:00.700
If you've taken like Spanish
II or something in high school,

01:21:00.700 --> 01:21:02.992
it's nice to practice a little
because that stuff tends

01:21:02.992 --> 01:21:06.550
to come back quickly if you
practice and not if you don't.

01:21:06.550 --> 01:21:09.370
And I will start speaking
a little bit of Spanglish.

01:21:09.370 --> 01:21:11.540
I have taken all of
Spanish I at MIT,

01:21:11.540 --> 01:21:14.110
so I am not your Spanish expert.

01:21:14.110 --> 01:21:17.982
But I have spent, by now over
the course of different weeks,

01:21:17.982 --> 01:21:19.690
probably a couple of
months in Nicaragua.

01:21:19.690 --> 01:21:21.107
So my Spanish is
a bit better when

01:21:21.107 --> 01:21:24.460
I talk about technical things
than I know about in Spanish

01:21:24.460 --> 01:21:26.800
than just general conversation.

01:21:26.800 --> 01:21:29.350
But, yeah, so I'm not expecting
you to be a Spanish expert

01:21:29.350 --> 01:21:31.570
through a technical class
because that would be

01:21:31.570 --> 01:21:32.950
ludicrous, and I'm not, myself.

01:21:32.950 --> 01:21:36.530
But it is helpful to know a
little bit of the language.

01:21:36.530 --> 01:21:40.420
Finally, just one thing is one
of the things you picked up

01:21:40.420 --> 01:21:42.820
is a Muddy card, which
you may or may not

01:21:42.820 --> 01:21:44.680
have encountered at MIT.

01:21:44.680 --> 01:21:46.630
And it's just a way
to give feedback

01:21:46.630 --> 01:21:48.970
to me about what
you understood, what

01:21:48.970 --> 01:21:51.940
you valued about the class, what
you didn't understand, hated,

01:21:51.940 --> 01:21:54.160
completely anonymously.

01:21:54.160 --> 01:21:57.410
And it allows me to improve
my teaching in the class.

01:21:57.410 --> 01:21:59.080
I am finishing up my PhD.

01:21:59.080 --> 01:22:00.500
I aspire to be a professor.

01:22:00.500 --> 01:22:03.610
So I really want to improve and
appreciate your help in that

01:22:03.610 --> 01:22:06.215
and appreciate
constructive criticism.

01:22:06.215 --> 01:22:08.090
It doesn't even have to
be that constructive.

01:22:08.090 --> 01:22:11.193
It's useful for me, and it's
anonymous, so you can be blunt.

01:22:11.193 --> 01:22:13.360
Or if there's something you
love, it's great to hear

01:22:13.360 --> 01:22:16.000
that too, just to know
what to keep in the class.

01:22:16.000 --> 01:22:20.900
But I really appreciate you
taking the time to do that.

01:22:20.900 --> 01:22:23.860
And then we also have similar
stuff with the readings,

01:22:23.860 --> 01:22:26.350
just getting feedback
online on the forums.

01:22:26.350 --> 01:22:29.737
Do you guys have any
questions about the syllabus

01:22:29.737 --> 01:22:30.820
and what I just went over?

01:22:36.190 --> 01:22:38.320
OK, cool.

01:22:38.320 --> 01:22:44.110
So in order to get
into the class,

01:22:44.110 --> 01:22:47.980
because it is a class where
you get to travel to Nicaragua,

01:22:47.980 --> 01:22:50.590
there's a big commitment
in terms of we're

01:22:50.590 --> 01:22:52.270
working with real
people in Nicaragua.

01:22:52.270 --> 01:22:56.950
And so we want to make sure
that you're here to really work

01:22:56.950 --> 01:23:02.440
and not just to fill out your
class requirements or whatever.

01:23:02.440 --> 01:23:05.298
We do have a strict
"what you have to do."

01:23:05.298 --> 01:23:07.090
So one is a student
profile, where you just

01:23:07.090 --> 01:23:08.320
tell me about yourself.

01:23:08.320 --> 01:23:10.720
That's online,
linked to on Stellar.

01:23:10.720 --> 01:23:13.570
And the other thing
is a DIY lantern.

01:23:13.570 --> 01:23:17.680
So I'm going to give you all
an LED and a 9-volt battery.

01:23:17.680 --> 01:23:19.450
You can pick up a
resistor, and you

01:23:19.450 --> 01:23:23.410
need to figure out what resistor
you need from the Edgerton

01:23:23.410 --> 01:23:27.190
Center, and this
is all online too.

01:23:27.190 --> 01:23:29.140
But it's room 4409.

01:23:29.140 --> 01:23:30.340
This is the combo.

01:23:30.340 --> 01:23:32.200
The resistors are on
your right, so you

01:23:32.200 --> 01:23:33.908
need to figure out
what your resistor is.

01:23:33.908 --> 01:23:36.940
I would recommend checking
that the resistor you pull out

01:23:36.940 --> 01:23:39.370
of the drawer is actually the
right resistor by looking up

01:23:39.370 --> 01:23:42.370
the resistor code and doing
the work because the Edgerton

01:23:42.370 --> 01:23:45.700
Center is a student lab, and so
resistors tend to get mixed up.

01:23:45.700 --> 01:23:48.250
And if you pick a
wildly wrong resistor,

01:23:48.250 --> 01:23:52.010
you will blow your LED, or
your device won't work at all.

01:23:52.010 --> 01:23:56.020
But on the flip side,
the V equals IR equation,

01:23:56.020 --> 01:23:59.825
in terms of how flexible an
LED is, is relatively flexible.

01:23:59.825 --> 01:24:01.450
So if you can't find
the exact resistor

01:24:01.450 --> 01:24:04.780
you've calculated the value
for, go up or down 10% or 15%,

01:24:04.780 --> 01:24:06.470
and you'll be fine.

01:24:06.470 --> 01:24:09.910
So anyway, so you're trying
to build a lantern out

01:24:09.910 --> 01:24:13.330
of found materials, that
resistor, the 9-volt battery,

01:24:13.330 --> 01:24:14.530
and the LED.

01:24:14.530 --> 01:24:17.570
And what I care about
is, does it function?

01:24:17.570 --> 01:24:19.120
So does it turn on?

01:24:19.120 --> 01:24:20.900
Is it possible to
turn it on and off?

01:24:20.900 --> 01:24:24.290
And can I figure out that switch
in a reasonable amount of time?

01:24:24.290 --> 01:24:27.190
Aesthetics-- does
it look decent?

01:24:27.190 --> 01:24:28.900
Did you use
materials creatively?

01:24:28.900 --> 01:24:33.190
So if you found a
lantern at EMS or REI

01:24:33.190 --> 01:24:36.020
tonight and bought it and
just swapped out the internals

01:24:36.020 --> 01:24:38.020
with this, that would not
be a very creative use

01:24:38.020 --> 01:24:39.550
of found materials.

01:24:39.550 --> 01:24:41.200
So I'm hoping that
you actually find

01:24:41.200 --> 01:24:43.840
stuff that's recycled rather.

01:24:43.840 --> 01:24:45.730
And then maximizing
light-- is it actually

01:24:45.730 --> 01:24:46.720
useful as a lantern?

01:24:46.720 --> 01:24:48.550
So those are the criteria.

01:24:48.550 --> 01:24:50.080
And you can bring
the lantern back

01:24:50.080 --> 01:24:51.820
to just the room down the hall.

01:24:51.820 --> 01:24:54.507
It's E34-211.

01:24:54.507 --> 01:24:56.590
And just placed it on a
desk with a piece of paper

01:24:56.590 --> 01:24:59.290
that has your name, a really
quick sketch of the lantern

01:24:59.290 --> 01:25:02.620
so that we remember what it is
when we give the lanterns back

01:25:02.620 --> 01:25:05.410
to you, and just a
really fast explanation

01:25:05.410 --> 01:25:06.940
of how you sized
the resistor so we

01:25:06.940 --> 01:25:09.670
know that you understand
that aspect of what we just

01:25:09.670 --> 01:25:10.903
