01:25:35.680 --> 01:25:37.920
AMY BANZAERT: So I'm sure
you can find some materials

01:25:37.920 --> 01:25:40.410
downstairs.

01:25:40.410 --> 01:25:43.170
Downstairs, there's a--
not in the engine lab.

01:25:43.170 --> 01:25:45.372
The engine lab, you
can just get resistors.

01:25:45.372 --> 01:25:46.830
And we actually
intentionally tried

01:25:46.830 --> 01:25:48.872
to make this class a little
short so there's time

01:25:48.872 --> 01:25:52.740
to help you start this project.

01:25:52.740 --> 01:25:57.450
So downstairs, there's
the D-Lab shop.

01:25:57.450 --> 01:26:00.630
And there's some scrap materials
in there that you can scrounge.

01:26:00.630 --> 01:26:04.770
Or the recycling bins in
dorms, at least back when I was

01:26:04.770 --> 01:26:07.080
a student here-- an
undergrad student--

01:26:07.080 --> 01:26:11.230
were pretty full of wild
things that might be useful.

01:26:11.230 --> 01:26:13.920
And MIT trash cans are amazing.

01:26:13.920 --> 01:26:15.750
There's so much
there, just walking

01:26:15.750 --> 01:26:17.640
the halls, where people
have piles of stuff

01:26:17.640 --> 01:26:18.723
that they're throwing out.

01:26:18.723 --> 01:26:21.477
There's all sorts of
stuff that you can grab.

01:26:21.477 --> 01:26:23.560
But, yeah, the idea is to
just use found materials

01:26:23.560 --> 01:26:25.320
because when you're
in the field,

01:26:25.320 --> 01:26:27.628
you're often just looking
around in the trash heaps.

01:26:27.628 --> 01:26:29.670
And, yes, the trash heaps
in developing countries

01:26:29.670 --> 01:26:34.320
and some regions are really
more useful than in other places

01:26:34.320 --> 01:26:36.487
than here for getting trash.

01:26:36.487 --> 01:26:38.070
People are also more
creative, so they

01:26:38.070 --> 01:26:40.660
tend to use up all their trash,
so there's not much left.

01:26:40.660 --> 01:26:42.670
So it's a trade-off.

01:26:42.670 --> 01:26:45.150
So in some respects, we
have worse trash piles,

01:26:45.150 --> 01:26:47.880
and in other respects, we
have better trash piles.

01:26:47.880 --> 01:26:49.800
But the idea is
to make a lantern

01:26:49.800 --> 01:26:54.660
with trash and the
components that I give you.

01:26:54.660 --> 01:26:56.710
Do you have any other
questions about it?

01:26:56.710 --> 01:26:57.210
Yeah.

01:26:57.210 --> 01:26:59.537
AUDIENCE: Can we use other
electronic components,

01:26:59.537 --> 01:27:00.120
like a switch?

01:27:00.120 --> 01:27:02.980
Or do we just get creative
for those as well?

01:27:02.980 --> 01:27:12.000
AMY BANZAERT: So you have
a design trade-off here.

01:27:12.000 --> 01:27:14.970
I want it to function,
which might push you

01:27:14.970 --> 01:27:17.100
toward using a switch.

01:27:17.100 --> 01:27:18.950
And I want you to
be creative, which

01:27:18.950 --> 01:27:20.700
might push you toward
using something that

01:27:20.700 --> 01:27:22.110
isn't a traditional switch.

01:27:22.110 --> 01:27:23.890
And that's really your call.

01:27:23.890 --> 01:27:26.820
And so if you decide that
the switch is the right call

01:27:26.820 --> 01:27:28.740
because you're going
to value functionality,

01:27:28.740 --> 01:27:30.840
maybe you do
something else that's

01:27:30.840 --> 01:27:33.100
a creative use of materials
in a different realm

01:27:33.100 --> 01:27:35.610
so that you try to
meet all things.

01:27:35.610 --> 01:27:38.280
But in every design
process, there's

01:27:38.280 --> 01:27:40.320
going to be direct conflicts.

01:27:40.320 --> 01:27:42.420
And I need it to be
super lightweight,

01:27:42.420 --> 01:27:46.110
and I need to have
a huge battery pack.

01:27:46.110 --> 01:27:48.090
That's a classic
design conflict,

01:27:48.090 --> 01:27:49.830
and I'm trying to
reproduce that here.

01:27:49.830 --> 01:27:51.205
AUDIENCE: And we
